1
00:00:03,712 --> 00:00:05,919
Hello , this is Steve Haberman .

2
00:00:06,006 --> 00:00:08,418
I 've written books such as Chronicles of Terror ,

3
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:11,422
about the history of horror films in the 20th century ,

4
00:00:11,512 --> 00:00:15,130
as well as movies like Mel Brooks ' Dracula : Dead and Loving It .

5
00:00:15,224 --> 00:00:17,181
And it 's my privilege to guide you

6
00:00:17,267 --> 00:00:19,352
through the first supernatural horror movie

7
00:00:19,436 --> 00:00:23,020
of the sound era , Tod Browning 's Dracula .

8
00:00:23,106 --> 00:00:27,145
Along the way , I 'd like to celebrate the many strengths of this haunting film ,

9
00:00:27,236 --> 00:00:30,774
and challenge some of the recent critical discourse about it .

10
00:00:30,864 --> 00:00:34,357
As Orson Welles once said , " Primitive man never insisted "

11
00:00:34,451 --> 00:00:37,443
" that someone in a cave get up and tell a tale . "

12
00:00:37,538 --> 00:00:40,576
" Rather , a single Neanderthal stood before the tribe "

13
00:00:40,666 --> 00:00:45,331
" and said , ' I 'm going to tell you a story ' , and the others listened . "

14
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:49,163
Welles was accurately describing the nature of show business .

15
00:00:49,258 --> 00:00:53,297
One artist with the talent and ego to demand attention ,

16
00:00:53,387 --> 00:00:55,719
tells the stories that interest him

17
00:00:55,806 --> 00:00:59,015
and creates an appetite in the audience for more .

18
00:00:59,101 --> 00:01:02,435
Carl Laemmle Jr. insisted on making a film of Dracula

19
00:01:02,521 --> 00:01:05,513
just because he enjoyed Tales of the Macabre .

20
00:01:05,607 --> 00:01:08,474
Thus , the horror film became a commercial genre .

21
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:15,739
The golden age of American horror that began with the tremendous success

22
00:01:15,826 --> 00:01:19,785
of Dracula in 1931 , didn 't introduce such films ,

23
00:01:19,871 --> 00:01:24,331
but it did signal an unprecedented peak of commercial popularity .

24
00:01:24,418 --> 00:01:26,580
Just consider the novelty at the time .

25
00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:28,536
Morbid supernatural stories

26
00:01:28,630 --> 00:01:32,294
presented with the technical facility of early sound films .

27
00:01:32,384 --> 00:01:36,378
No wonder Dracula drew large , curious audiences eager to escape

28
00:01:36,471 --> 00:01:40,135
from the harsh realities of a worldwide economic depression .

29
00:01:41,018 --> 00:01:43,186
By the way , if you want to see the superiority

30
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:45,136
of Tod Browning 's directorial style

31
00:01:45,230 --> 00:01:48,143
over George Melford 's on the recently celebrated ,

32
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:51,066
but actually quite inferior Spanish version ,

33
00:01:51,153 --> 00:01:53,019
just look at these early scenes .

34
00:01:53,113 --> 00:01:56,902
Where Browning covers with close-ups and cuts for emphasis ,

35
00:01:56,992 --> 00:02:00,610
Melford simply stages master shots and lets them play out .

36
00:02:01,413 --> 00:02:02,914
Just think about the difficulties

37
00:02:02,998 --> 00:02:06,491
of presenting supernatural horror in 1931 .

38
00:02:06,585 --> 00:02:08,792
Filmmakers afraid to offend ,

39
00:02:08,879 --> 00:02:11,792
actors untrained for the intimacy of the microphone ,

40
00:02:11,882 --> 00:02:14,169
primitive modes of sound recording ,

41
00:02:14,259 --> 00:02:16,717
and the moral condemnation from a large section

42
00:02:16,803 --> 00:02:18,919
of civic and artistic authority .

43
00:02:19,014 --> 00:02:21,140
Even so , many of the works from this period ,

44
00:02:21,224 --> 00:02:26,435
including Dracula , remain powerful , definitive examples of the genre .

45
00:02:26,521 --> 00:02:28,808
This was due largely to brilliant decisions

46
00:02:28,899 --> 00:02:33,143
in the hiring of actors , writers , directors and technical personnel .

47
00:02:33,236 --> 00:02:35,898
The combination of timing and circumstances

48
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:38,606
resulted in a novel approach to the subject matter ,

49
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:42,944
which was both literal and reticent and gave films like Dracula

50
00:02:43,038 --> 00:02:45,530
a haunting , unforgettable quality .

51
00:02:45,624 --> 00:02:48,293
They 're not like the horror films from any other period ,

52
00:02:48,377 --> 00:02:50,960
and that 's their strength and their value .

53
00:02:51,838 --> 00:02:54,296
Some repetitious dialogue was cut from this scene

54
00:02:54,383 --> 00:02:57,717
in Browning 's version , keeping the focus on Renfield .

55
00:02:57,803 --> 00:02:58,964
Melford , of course ,

56
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:01,431
lets the entire scene play in the Spanish version ,

57
00:03:01,515 --> 00:03:04,382
slowing down the action and blunting the foreshadowing .

58
00:03:04,476 --> 00:03:05,932
Well , I 'm sorry , but ...

59
00:03:06,019 --> 00:03:07,475
Audiences of the 1930s

60
00:03:07,562 --> 00:03:10,520
may have initially flocked to Dracula for its novelty ,

61
00:03:10,607 --> 00:03:13,318
but the film continued to fascinate throughout the decades

62
00:03:13,402 --> 00:03:16,269
because it tapped into subconscious anxieties .

63
00:03:16,363 --> 00:03:18,274
The public was ready for the emotional

64
00:03:18,365 --> 00:03:21,027
and intellectual challenges of horror films .

65
00:03:21,118 --> 00:03:23,780
They 're complacent reliance on economic security

66
00:03:23,912 --> 00:03:26,495
had been shattered by the Great Depression .

67
00:03:26,581 --> 00:03:31,917
Close contact with death and disfigurement was still a fresh memory from World War I.

68
00:03:32,003 --> 00:03:35,298
In addition to all of that , religious certainty had been questioned

69
00:03:35,382 --> 00:03:37,248
by recent works of science .

70
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:40,793
The Great Depression caused fear of impending chaos

71
00:03:40,887 --> 00:03:43,845
even in a society as prosperous as the United States .

72
00:03:44,474 --> 00:03:47,933
After the stock market crash in October 1929 ,

73
00:03:48,019 --> 00:03:51,137
the unthinkable occurred with depressing regularity .

74
00:03:51,231 --> 00:03:55,316
Banks and companies folded , families lost their ability to make money ,

75
00:03:55,402 --> 00:03:58,815
and the free enterprise system seemed about to collapse .

76
00:03:58,905 --> 00:04:03,149
Moviemakers during the Great Depression vented their terror of social chaos

77
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:05,450
in talkie horrors like Dracula .

78
00:04:05,537 --> 00:04:06,902
Think about the story .

79
00:04:06,997 --> 00:04:12,413
An insidious evil silently attempts to destroy a family from within ,

80
00:04:12,502 --> 00:04:16,837
ingratiating itself and then literally sucking it dry of blood .

81
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:19,460
It doesn 't take much imagination to see the Count

82
00:04:19,551 --> 00:04:21,292
as a metaphor for the Depression .

83
00:04:21,386 --> 00:04:22,672
Wait .

84
00:04:22,763 --> 00:04:26,722
Just before this bleak economic situation , the industrialized world enjoyed

85
00:04:26,808 --> 00:04:31,427
a rush of scientific progress , introducing radios , skyscrapers ,

86
00:04:31,521 --> 00:04:35,731
affordable automobiles , sound films , transatlantic plane travel ,

87
00:04:35,817 --> 00:04:38,275
and electrical conveniences for the home .

88
00:04:38,361 --> 00:04:42,571
This dizzying sprint into the future from a recent " horse and buggy " past

89
00:04:42,657 --> 00:04:46,525
produced both cultural excitement and anxiety .

90
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:48,987
Horror movies with cautionary metaphysicians

91
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:50,787
like Van Helsing and Dracula ,

92
00:04:50,874 --> 00:04:52,956
express this distrust of progress ,

93
00:04:53,043 --> 00:04:55,626
and its blindness toward biblical morality .

94
00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:58,631
Further challenging old tenets of behavior

95
00:04:58,715 --> 00:05:03,004
were the relatively new insights of psychoanalysis and evolution .

96
00:05:03,094 --> 00:05:06,337
Maybe man was not in control of his own mind .

97
00:05:06,431 --> 00:05:09,969
Maybe we were victims of our impure desires .

98
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:13,269
With its themes of repression , guilt , and lack of control ,

99
00:05:13,355 --> 00:05:15,813
especially in the Renfield and Mina characters ,

100
00:05:15,899 --> 00:05:19,233
Dracula fascinated viewers primed by a nodding acquaintance

101
00:05:19,319 --> 00:05:21,069
with the works of Freud and Darwin .

102
00:05:22,489 --> 00:05:25,402
This scene , so beautifully staged by Browning

103
00:05:25,492 --> 00:05:27,199
and shot by Karl Freund ,

104
00:05:27,285 --> 00:05:30,368
is completely incompetent in the Spanish version .

105
00:05:30,455 --> 00:05:32,867
Bits and pieces of outtakes from Browning 's scene

106
00:05:32,958 --> 00:05:34,619
are cut together by Melford .

107
00:05:34,709 --> 00:05:39,829
His scene omits the stunning push-in on Dracula staring at the camera .

108
00:05:39,923 --> 00:05:42,130
Instead , Dracula rises from his coffin ,

109
00:05:42,217 --> 00:05:45,175
staged without depth , against one of the stone walls

110
00:05:45,262 --> 00:05:47,799
in a ludicrous , glowing billow of fog .

111
00:05:47,889 --> 00:05:51,052
By making the scene so literally supernatural and obvious ,

112
00:05:51,142 --> 00:05:55,887
Melford robs it of the morbid poetry of Browning 's version .

113
00:05:55,981 --> 00:06:00,521
Browning consistently treats the supernatural in an understated manner ,

114
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:05,650
making it more believable , and maintaining a quiet , uncanny atmosphere .

115
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:09,904
Master cinematographer Karl Freund , who photographed silent horror classics ,

116
00:06:09,995 --> 00:06:12,157
The Golem and Metropolis in Germany ,

117
00:06:12,247 --> 00:06:15,865
moves his camera ominously at just the right moments .

118
00:06:15,959 --> 00:06:19,543
He lights the magnificent sets designed by Charles D. Hall

119
00:06:19,629 --> 00:06:24,169
to look like etchings by the 19th century illustrator Gustave Doré .

120
00:06:24,259 --> 00:06:27,297
Even the model of the castle exterior , which Freund designed ,

121
00:06:27,387 --> 00:06:31,631
and the rocky surrounding countryside matched the Doré style .

122
00:06:31,725 --> 00:06:33,090
Here at the Borgo Pass ,

123
00:06:33,184 --> 00:06:36,802
Melford 's version , again , suffers in comparison to Browning 's .

124
00:06:36,938 --> 00:06:41,227
Most importantly , there 's no fog at the Borgo Pass in the Spanish scene ,

125
00:06:41,318 --> 00:06:46,358
Melford also omits the memorable special effect of Dracula 's glowing eyes .

126
00:06:46,448 --> 00:06:49,986
As early as 1929 , though Universal hadn 't yet purchased

127
00:06:50,076 --> 00:06:53,990
the rights to the 1897 book , and 1927 stage play

128
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:55,662
of Bram Stoker 's Dracula ,

129
00:06:55,749 --> 00:06:58,537
their young associate producer , Paul Kohner ,

130
00:06:58,627 --> 00:07:00,753
planted press releases about the planned film

131
00:07:00,837 --> 00:07:05,126
with Conrad Veidt playing Dracula under the direction of Paul Leni .

132
00:07:05,216 --> 00:07:07,457
In his ambition to rise up the studio ladder ,

133
00:07:07,552 --> 00:07:11,511
Kohner lobbied for this package , having worked with both star and director

134
00:07:11,598 --> 00:07:15,466
on the lavish period silent , The Man Who Laughs in 1928 .

135
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:17,597
But Kohner 's scheme was not to be .

136
00:07:17,687 --> 00:07:21,021
Paul Lent 's infected tooth resulted in fatal blood poisoning

137
00:07:21,107 --> 00:07:23,314
in September of 1929 .

138
00:07:23,401 --> 00:07:25,108
Conrad Veidt returned to Germany

139
00:07:25,195 --> 00:07:29,780
rather than risk talking pictures in America with his imperfect English .

140
00:07:29,866 --> 00:07:32,118
Kohner 's producing ambitions were further crippled

141
00:07:32,202 --> 00:07:34,614
when Universal President Carl Laemmle

142
00:07:34,704 --> 00:07:38,743
appointed his 21-year-old son , Julius , head of production .

143
00:07:38,833 --> 00:07:41,700
Julius promptly changed his name to Carl Laemmle Jr .

144
00:07:41,795 --> 00:07:45,413
Out of gratitude and to maintain studio continuity .

145
00:07:45,507 --> 00:07:47,839
Laemmle demoted Kohner from major projects ,

146
00:07:47,926 --> 00:07:50,839
allowing him only to produce the foreign language versions

147
00:07:50,929 --> 00:07:55,048
of Universal talkies such as the Spanish version of Dracula .

148
00:07:55,725 --> 00:07:59,093
Carl Jr. loved Gothic Tales of Terror , and the play of Dracula ,

149
00:07:59,187 --> 00:08:02,054
starring Bela Lugosi , had been performed in Los Angeles

150
00:08:02,148 --> 00:08:04,389
to enormous success the previous summer .

151
00:08:05,026 --> 00:08:08,439
Laemmle Jr. decided that for the movie , Lon Chaney would play

152
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:12,774
both the vampire Count Dracula , and his nemesis , Professor Van Helsing ,

153
00:08:12,867 --> 00:08:17,111
much as Chaney had done in Tod Browning 's silent MGM Dracula knock-off ,

154
00:08:17,205 --> 00:08:20,038
London After Midnight , in 1927 .

155
00:08:20,125 --> 00:08:24,244
Several problems immediately loomed for Laemmle Jr . 's ambitious project .

156
00:08:24,337 --> 00:08:28,046
Lon Chaney developed pernicious anemia following a tonsillectomy .

157
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:30,465
He barely survived a deadly case of pneumonia

158
00:08:30,552 --> 00:08:35,672
caught while filming Thunder on location , the last picture on his old MGM contract ,

159
00:08:35,765 --> 00:08:38,976
Universal tried to lure him to sign a three-picture deal with them to star

160
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:43,600
in a remake of Browning 's Outside the Law , the film of Dracula , and a sequel

161
00:08:43,690 --> 00:08:46,808
to The Phantom of the Opera called Return of the Phantom .

162
00:08:46,901 --> 00:08:49,487
The incision in Chaney 's throat from the tonsillectomy

163
00:08:49,571 --> 00:08:51,437
refused to heal properly .

164
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:53,282
Chaney , always insecure about money ,

165
00:08:53,366 --> 00:08:56,358
chose to sign on with MGM for another five years .

166
00:08:56,911 --> 00:09:00,456
His first talking picture was scheduled to be a French foreign legion adventure

167
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:03,623
called The Bugle Sounds , to be directed by Jack Conway .

168
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:06,998
But Chaney 's fragile health prevented him from going on location

169
00:09:07,088 --> 00:09:11,423
to portray another hard-boiled sergeant as he had in Tell It to the Marines .

170
00:09:11,509 --> 00:09:15,878
Instead , MGM executive Irving Thalberg remade Browning 's The Unholy Three

171
00:09:15,972 --> 00:09:19,135
with Conway replacing Browning at the helm .

172
00:09:19,225 --> 00:09:23,219
Chaney talked for the first time on-screen , and in five different voices .

173
00:09:23,313 --> 00:09:25,600
The film wrapped in the late spring of 1930

174
00:09:25,690 --> 00:09:29,809
and was released on July 6th to great critical and commercial success .

175
00:09:29,903 --> 00:09:32,736
But two weeks before its debut on June 23rd ,

176
00:09:32,822 --> 00:09:35,735
Chaney 's doctors diagnosed him with lung cancer .

177
00:09:35,825 --> 00:09:38,533
Although MGM publicity underplayed Chaney 's illness

178
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:40,782
to prevent MGM stock from dropping ,

179
00:09:40,872 --> 00:09:45,491
by July , the studio suspended Cheney 's contract due to his incapacity .

180
00:09:49,923 --> 00:09:52,711
In the Spanish version , Melford stages this scene

181
00:09:52,801 --> 00:09:55,919
as indicated in the script , but blunts the effect .

182
00:09:56,012 --> 00:10:00,552
The screenplay calls for a crane shot to go from Renfield swatting at a bat

183
00:10:00,642 --> 00:10:03,384
to a close view of Dracula on the staircase .

184
00:10:03,478 --> 00:10:07,062
Unfortunately , the crane move in the Spanish version is bumpy

185
00:10:07,148 --> 00:10:09,435
and doesn 't seem to get any closer to Dracula

186
00:10:09,526 --> 00:10:11,517
after the initial reveal .

187
00:10:11,611 --> 00:10:14,729
Browning 's understated introduction is far more effective

188
00:10:14,823 --> 00:10:17,110
and makes a metaphorical point .

189
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:21,444
Browning introduces Dracula emerging from the darkness of the castle

190
00:10:21,538 --> 00:10:25,327
as if he were one of the vermin we 've seen crawling out of the cracks .

191
00:10:25,416 --> 00:10:28,408
Like the possums , bugs , bats , and armadillos ,

192
00:10:28,503 --> 00:10:31,666
Dracula is also one of the children of the night .

193
00:10:31,756 --> 00:10:36,626
By the way , rats in films were considered in wretched poor taste in 1930 .

194
00:10:36,719 --> 00:10:39,086
Only five years earlier , Universal cut a shot

195
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:41,672
of swarms of rats emerging from the cellars

196
00:10:41,808 --> 00:10:44,351
herded by the rat catcher in The Phantom of the Opera

197
00:10:44,435 --> 00:10:47,348
because the preview audience was so disgusted .

198
00:10:47,438 --> 00:10:49,850
Browning seems to have found even uglier animals

199
00:10:49,941 --> 00:10:53,104
for Castle Dracula , but tastefully omits actual rats .

200
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:54,945
In the Spanish version ,

201
00:10:55,029 --> 00:10:57,396
Melford allows Renfield to climb the stairs

202
00:10:57,532 --> 00:11:02,197
and play his dialogue with Dracula face to face in a prosaic two shot .

203
00:11:02,328 --> 00:11:05,741
This is far less effective than having Renfield look up at Dracula

204
00:11:05,832 --> 00:11:07,664
and cutting between the two ,

205
00:11:07,750 --> 00:11:11,084
isolating Dracula in the shot for his most famous lines

206
00:11:11,171 --> 00:11:14,539
and shooting Renfield like a vulnerable victim from above .

207
00:11:14,632 --> 00:11:18,717
Of course , Carlos Villarías as Dracula is also no match for Bela Lugosi

208
00:11:18,803 --> 00:11:21,636
in terms of magnetic personality or acting .

209
00:11:26,311 --> 00:11:29,429
When Laemmle Jr. learned of Chaney 's suspension from MGM ,

210
00:11:29,522 --> 00:11:33,516
he considered offering Chaney a personal , rather than a studio contract .

211
00:11:33,610 --> 00:11:36,362
Chaney could have freely accepted , since his agreement with MGM

212
00:11:36,446 --> 00:11:39,689
only bound him exclusively for silent films .

213
00:11:39,782 --> 00:11:43,275
On July 22nd , MGM 's lawyers warned Laemmle Jr .

214
00:11:43,369 --> 00:11:44,905
Not to attempt such a deal

215
00:11:44,996 --> 00:11:47,704
since it violated motion picture business ethics ,

216
00:11:47,790 --> 00:11:50,828
and could even be ruled illegal in light of a lawsuit .

217
00:11:51,336 --> 00:11:54,078
Another obstacle facing Laemmle Jr . 's Dracula

218
00:11:54,172 --> 00:11:56,504
was the objection of his own father .

219
00:11:56,591 --> 00:12:00,050
Carl Sr. didn 't share his enthusiasm for morbid stories ,

220
00:12:00,136 --> 00:12:02,844
even though he 'd scored two of his biggest silent hits

221
00:12:02,972 --> 00:12:04,265
with the Chaney horror vehicles ,

222
00:12:04,349 --> 00:12:07,592
The Hunchback of Notre Dame and The Phantom of the Opera .

223
00:12:07,685 --> 00:12:10,729
Universal 's story department readers agreed with the elder Laemmle ,

224
00:12:10,813 --> 00:12:13,851
writing negative reaction to the novel as movie material

225
00:12:13,942 --> 00:12:18,436
as far back as 1915 , when Universal first considered the property .

226
00:12:18,529 --> 00:12:22,318
Even after the book had been successfully transferred to the London stage ,

227
00:12:22,408 --> 00:12:26,026
a reader named Steve Miranda wrote in 1927 ,

228
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:30,239
" While this may have a fantastic opening on stage and be very engrossing

229
00:12:30,333 --> 00:12:31,869
" for those who like the weird ,

230
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:35,123
" I cannot possibly see how it 's going to make a motion picture . "

231
00:12:35,213 --> 00:12:38,626
" It is blood , blood , blood , kill , and everything that would cause "

232
00:12:38,716 --> 00:12:43,085
" any average human being to revolt or seek a convenient railing . "

233
00:12:43,388 --> 00:12:46,238
" Sorry , but I cannot see where there is anything in this . "

234
00:12:46,599 --> 00:12:49,466
Another Universal reader named George Mitchell Jr .

235
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:52,723
Wrote that , " We all like to see ugly things .

236
00:12:52,814 --> 00:12:56,148
" We 're all attracted to a certain extent to that which is hideous .

237
00:12:56,234 --> 00:12:58,944
" For instance , the big appeal in The Phantom of the Opera .

238
00:12:59,028 --> 00:13:02,692
" But when it passes a certain point , the attraction dies , "

239
00:13:02,782 --> 00:13:05,865
" and we suffer a feeling of repulsion and nausea . "

240
00:13:05,952 --> 00:13:07,943
" Dracula passes beyond the point "

241
00:13:08,037 --> 00:13:11,530
" of what the average person can stand or cares to stand . "

242
00:13:13,209 --> 00:13:18,079
Laemmle Jr. persisted with his father , repeatedly clashing with him over Dracula .

243
00:13:18,172 --> 00:13:22,086
Carl Jr. pointed to the triumphant success of his own first major effort ,

244
00:13:22,176 --> 00:13:25,885
the downbeat but compelling film of Erich Maria Remarque 's

245
00:13:25,972 --> 00:13:29,385
World War I novel , All Quiet on the Western Front .

246
00:13:29,475 --> 00:13:34,515
He also argued that other studios such as Fox , Pathé , Colombia , and MGM

247
00:13:34,605 --> 00:13:39,145
considered adapting Dracula in light of its recent success as a play .

248
00:13:39,235 --> 00:13:42,102
Finally , Laemmle Sr. reluctantly agreed ,

249
00:13:42,196 --> 00:13:45,439
but only with the box office guarantee that Lon Chaney ,

250
00:13:45,533 --> 00:13:49,071
the number one male attraction in the country , play the vampire .

251
00:13:49,829 --> 00:13:51,319
In January 1930 ,

252
00:13:51,414 --> 00:13:54,623
before securing the rights to either Dracula or Lon Chaney ,

253
00:13:54,709 --> 00:14:00,295
Universal signed Chaney 's MGM director , Tod Browning , to a five-year contract .

254
00:14:00,381 --> 00:14:03,590
Browning had entered into new contract negotiations with MGM

255
00:14:03,676 --> 00:14:07,260
in the spring of 1929 , and his first Chaney project

256
00:14:07,347 --> 00:14:10,635
was scheduled to be a , quote , weird story of tropical life

257
00:14:10,725 --> 00:14:13,763
and jungle voodoo , unquote , called The Sea Bat .

258
00:14:14,312 --> 00:14:18,021
Further progress on both the picture and Browning 's contract looked bleak

259
00:14:18,107 --> 00:14:19,723
as Chaney 's health deteriorated .

260
00:14:20,401 --> 00:14:22,062
Browning still had one more movie

261
00:14:22,153 --> 00:14:24,565
to deliver to MGM on his three-project contract

262
00:14:24,655 --> 00:14:28,273
that began with West of Zanzibar , and Where East Is East .

263
00:14:28,368 --> 00:14:31,486
MGM assigned him to make a talking and silent version

264
00:14:31,579 --> 00:14:36,790
of Ba yard Veiller 's 1917 stage melodrama , The Thirteenth Chair .

265
00:14:36,876 --> 00:14:39,618
Perhaps realizing that Chaney might never work again ,

266
00:14:39,712 --> 00:14:42,124
but still interested in doing a film of Dracula ,

267
00:14:42,215 --> 00:14:44,001
as he and Chaney had discussed ,

268
00:14:44,092 --> 00:14:47,835
Browning cast the New York stage Dracula , Bela Lugosi ,

269
00:14:47,929 --> 00:14:51,047
as a Scotland Yard inspector in The Thirteenth Chair .

270
00:14:51,682 --> 00:14:54,845
Bela Lugosi , along with Boris Karloff , would eventually become

271
00:14:54,936 --> 00:14:59,396
the most important star of horror movies in the first two decades of sound .

272
00:14:59,482 --> 00:15:02,315
He was born Béla Ferenc Dezső Blaskó

273
00:15:02,402 --> 00:15:05,770
on October 20th , 1882 , in Lugos , Hungary .

274
00:15:06,364 --> 00:15:08,901
His father was a baker-turned-banker ,

275
00:15:08,991 --> 00:15:11,574
and the son clashed with his authoritarian patriarch

276
00:15:11,661 --> 00:15:13,572
about becoming a professional actor .

277
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:16,788
Training at the Budapest Academy of Theatrical Arts ,

278
00:15:16,874 --> 00:15:21,243
Lugosi started appearing on the Hungarian stage in 1901 .

279
00:15:21,337 --> 00:15:25,251
The darkly handsome actor broke into silent films in 1915 ,

280
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:28,174
sometimes using the name of Arisztid Olt .

281
00:15:28,261 --> 00:15:31,549
In 1918 , during the collapse of the Hungarian monarchy ,

282
00:15:31,639 --> 00:15:33,724
and the establishment of a communist government ,

283
00:15:33,808 --> 00:15:36,345
Lugosi organized an actors union .

284
00:15:36,436 --> 00:15:38,768
When the communists were defeated in 1919 ,

285
00:15:38,855 --> 00:15:43,224
Lugosi was forced to flee to Germany because of his leftist politics .

286
00:15:43,317 --> 00:15:46,487
He appeared in a number of films in Germany , including as a butler

287
00:15:46,571 --> 00:15:49,780
in F.W. Murnau 's unauthorized adaptation

288
00:15:49,866 --> 00:15:53,075
of The Strange Case of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde .

289
00:15:53,161 --> 00:15:56,324
In 1921 , Lugosi immigrated to the United States

290
00:15:56,414 --> 00:15:59,156
and began acting on stage and in films .

291
00:15:59,250 --> 00:16:03,460
He eventually landed the role of Dracula on Broadway in 1927 .

292
00:16:03,546 --> 00:16:05,756
For two additional years , he toured with Dracula ,

293
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:11,131
appearing all over the United States , including in the film capital Los Angeles .

294
00:16:12,054 --> 00:16:14,921
In The Thirteenth Chair , Tod Browning obviously showcased

295
00:16:15,016 --> 00:16:17,007
Lugosi 's magnetic presence ,

296
00:16:17,101 --> 00:16:20,101
perhaps to promote him for the director 's version of Dracula .

297
00:16:20,438 --> 00:16:22,896
Browning 's stagy film of The Thirteenth Chair

298
00:16:22,982 --> 00:16:26,350
opened on Friday the 13th of December , 1929 .

299
00:16:26,444 --> 00:16:28,936
It netted a profit of $ 148,000 .

300
00:16:29,030 --> 00:16:31,740
But this was only half of what Browning 's recent pictures

301
00:16:31,824 --> 00:16:33,155
with Chaney had made .

302
00:16:33,618 --> 00:16:36,861
MGM did not renew its five-year association with Browning ,

303
00:16:36,954 --> 00:16:40,072
freeing the director to sign with Universal in January .

304
00:16:40,875 --> 00:16:43,127
Dracula may have been the reason for engaging Browning ,

305
00:16:43,211 --> 00:16:44,878
but Universal announced to the press

306
00:16:44,962 --> 00:16:47,214
that his first assignment would be to write and direct

307
00:16:47,298 --> 00:16:51,132
an original crime melodrama called The Scarlet Triangle .

308
00:16:51,219 --> 00:16:55,087
Universal soon canceled that project and replaced it with a talking remake

309
00:16:55,181 --> 00:16:58,594
of his 1920 hit for the studio , The Virgin of Stamboul .

310
00:16:59,101 --> 00:17:02,438
Then , The Virgin of Stamboul was canceled in favor of a talkie version

311
00:17:02,522 --> 00:17:05,810
of the studio 's Browning-Chaney silent , Outside the Law .

312
00:17:06,192 --> 00:17:09,570
Like many other silent directors , Browning initially found it difficult

313
00:17:09,654 --> 00:17:12,316
adapting to the new requirements of sound .

314
00:17:12,406 --> 00:17:15,239
The result was like MGM 's The Thirteenth Chair ,

315
00:17:15,326 --> 00:17:18,614
another stagy , poorly paced talking picture .

316
00:17:20,373 --> 00:17:23,365
In the Spanish version , Melford 's vampire brides

317
00:17:23,459 --> 00:17:26,622
attack Renfield and are far more ferocious .

318
00:17:26,712 --> 00:17:29,795
Unfortunately , they don 't match the wraith-like vampire women

319
00:17:29,882 --> 00:17:32,340
that Melford intercuts from the Browning version .

320
00:17:32,927 --> 00:17:36,138
Of course , many of the bloodier , more savage passages of the novel

321
00:17:36,222 --> 00:17:40,181
had to be eliminated or suggested so as not to offend audiences

322
00:17:40,268 --> 00:17:42,885
not yet used to such horrors on the screen .

323
00:17:42,979 --> 00:17:44,344
The sexy , voracious brides

324
00:17:44,438 --> 00:17:46,975
who attacked Dracula 's guest in Stoker 's novel ,

325
00:17:47,066 --> 00:17:49,568
were transformed into these speechless , hollow-eyed ,

326
00:17:49,652 --> 00:17:53,566
slowly gliding race that behave as if in a trance .

327
00:17:53,656 --> 00:17:55,522
Their sinister presence in the castle

328
00:17:55,616 --> 00:17:59,450
reinforced the unearthly , undead quality of Dracula .

329
00:17:59,537 --> 00:18:03,531
As in Stoker 's novel , Browning has Dracula wave away his brides .

330
00:18:03,624 --> 00:18:06,207
" This man belongs to me , " he says in the novel .

331
00:18:06,294 --> 00:18:09,036
But Browning 's silent staging is far more consistent

332
00:18:09,130 --> 00:18:12,623
with the quiet , eerie atmosphere he so carefully built .

333
00:18:14,594 --> 00:18:16,881
Finally , Universal formally assigned Browning

334
00:18:16,971 --> 00:18:20,259
to direct Dracula in July of 1930 .

335
00:18:20,349 --> 00:18:23,227
By this time , the property had been plagiarized for the screen

336
00:18:23,311 --> 00:18:28,101
in F.W. Murnau 's silent , Nosferatu , adapted three times for the stage ,

337
00:18:28,190 --> 00:18:30,442
and become the subject of much bitter litigation

338
00:18:30,526 --> 00:18:33,564
between Bram Stoker 's widow and various opportunists

339
00:18:33,654 --> 00:18:35,270
eager to exploit the subject .

340
00:18:35,740 --> 00:18:37,697
Universal finally managed to purchase

341
00:18:37,783 --> 00:18:41,242
the rights to the book and stage adaptation for $ 40,000

342
00:18:41,329 --> 00:18:43,286
in the summer of 1930 .

343
00:18:43,831 --> 00:18:47,167
Universal 's legal department had to deal with not only Florence Stoker ,

344
00:18:47,251 --> 00:18:50,414
but with playwrights Hamilton Deane and John L. Balderston .

345
00:18:51,130 --> 00:18:55,419
In 1924 , Florence Stoker licensed theatrical rights to Hamilton Deane ,

346
00:18:55,509 --> 00:18:59,503
who toured in the English provinces with his version for three years .

347
00:18:59,597 --> 00:19:02,464
As an actor-manager , Deane 's barnstorming technique

348
00:19:02,558 --> 00:19:04,424
was quite popular in the provinces ,

349
00:19:04,518 --> 00:19:07,306
but out of style on the sophisticated London stage .

350
00:19:07,855 --> 00:19:10,187
He decided to play the role of Van Helsing

351
00:19:10,274 --> 00:19:13,141
since the character had more speeches than Dracula .

352
00:19:13,235 --> 00:19:16,398
Deane 's wife , Dora Mary Patrick , played Mina .

353
00:19:16,489 --> 00:19:19,732
Despite his play 's immediate and lasting success on tour ,

354
00:19:19,825 --> 00:19:22,658
Deane feared risking the London theater critics ' raff

355
00:19:23,371 --> 00:19:27,615
Jose Levy , who successfully mounted Sybil Thorndike 's season of Grand Guignol

356
00:19:27,708 --> 00:19:29,293
at the Little Theater in the Adelphi ,

357
00:19:29,377 --> 00:19:32,290
convinced Deane that London craved sensational horror .

358
00:19:32,588 --> 00:19:36,547
Deane reluctantly opened Dracula at the Little in February 1927 ,

359
00:19:36,634 --> 00:19:40,298
and immediately struck gold despite the critics ' predicted hostility .

360
00:19:40,846 --> 00:19:44,430
Again , on board the ship , Browning chooses an understated approach ,

361
00:19:44,517 --> 00:19:47,227
avoiding the violence to the crew , called for in the script ,

362
00:19:47,311 --> 00:19:50,053
and letting the audience 's imagination do the work .

363
00:19:50,147 --> 00:19:52,764
The Spanish version of the scene is also effective .

364
00:19:52,858 --> 00:19:56,852
Not so much for Dracula 's grimacing , but for the insane laughter of Renfield .

365
00:19:57,029 --> 00:19:59,896
Glimpsed through a glowing porthole during the storm .

366
00:20:00,282 --> 00:20:03,365
Mrs. Stoker disliked Hamilton Deane 's adaptation ,

367
00:20:03,452 --> 00:20:06,365
which removed much of Dracula 's theatrical dialogue

368
00:20:06,455 --> 00:20:10,289
and reset most of the action in a cozy drawing room and bedroom .

369
00:20:10,376 --> 00:20:13,209
She commissioned and owned a far more faithful version

370
00:20:13,295 --> 00:20:16,708
from playwright Charles Morrell , which competed less successfully

371
00:20:16,799 --> 00:20:20,838
with Deane 's record-breaking West End run in September of 1927 .

372
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:23,712
American publisher and producer Horace Liveright

373
00:20:23,806 --> 00:20:26,218
saw the exploitation potential in Deane 's play ,

374
00:20:26,475 --> 00:20:29,263
but thought his writing below standard for Broadway .

375
00:20:29,520 --> 00:20:32,182
He hired John L. Balderston , who had co-written

376
00:20:32,273 --> 00:20:35,391
the successful supernatural romance , Berkeley Square ,

377
00:20:35,484 --> 00:20:38,234
to rework the dialogue and structure of Deane 's version .

378
00:20:38,738 --> 00:20:42,652
His rewrite of Deane 's Dracula eliminated the Texan female character ,

379
00:20:42,742 --> 00:20:45,575
Quincey Morris , who had been a man in Stoker 's novel .

380
00:20:45,995 --> 00:20:50,535
It also eliminated Lucy 's other suitor , Lord Godalming , and turned Dr. Seward

381
00:20:50,624 --> 00:20:54,288
from a young physician who loved and lost Lucy , into Mina 's father .

382
00:20:54,879 --> 00:20:57,712
It enlarge the parts of both Dracula and Renfield .

383
00:20:58,132 --> 00:21:00,133
The main setting changed from Harker 's home ,

384
00:21:00,217 --> 00:21:03,505
situated between Carfax and Seward Sanitarium ,

385
00:21:03,763 --> 00:21:06,095
to Seward 's home on his asylum grounds .

386
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:09,348
Balderston retained Deane 's comedy relief characters

387
00:21:09,435 --> 00:21:12,143
of Dr. Seward 's maid and Renfield 's attendant .

388
00:21:12,730 --> 00:21:15,188
Quite inexplicably , Balderston also switched

389
00:21:15,274 --> 00:21:17,474
the names of the characters of Lucy and Mina ,

390
00:21:17,610 --> 00:21:22,275
calling Dracula 's first British bride Mina , and Harker 's fiancée , Lucy .

391
00:21:22,656 --> 00:21:23,646
Don 't ask me .

392
00:21:24,241 --> 00:21:26,528
In both Deane 's and Balderston 's versions ,

393
00:21:26,619 --> 00:21:30,704
the Lucy character 's already buried and undead when the curtain rises .

394
00:21:30,790 --> 00:21:34,704
By eliminating characters and setting the action at Dr. Seward 's Sanitarium ,

395
00:21:34,794 --> 00:21:38,537
Balderston further streamline Deane 's already streamlined version

396
00:21:38,672 --> 00:21:40,834
of Stoker 's over-elaborate novel .

397
00:21:41,342 --> 00:21:44,505
British actor Raymond Huntley , Deane 's most popular Dracula ,

398
00:21:44,595 --> 00:21:47,337
balked at the $ 150-per-week salary

399
00:21:47,431 --> 00:21:50,344
offered by Liveright to reprise his role on Broadway .

400
00:21:50,935 --> 00:21:55,179
Instead , struggling refugee actor Bela Lugosi accepted even less ,

401
00:21:55,272 --> 00:21:57,263
and the rest was theatrical history .

402
00:21:57,942 --> 00:22:02,652
Seldom had an actor 's personality so enhanced and enriched a character .

403
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:07,239
Lugosi 's heavily accented , arrogant and ironic interpretation

404
00:22:07,326 --> 00:22:09,863
didn 't exactly match Stoker 's description ,

405
00:22:09,995 --> 00:22:13,204
but it 's supplanted it in the public 's imagination .

406
00:22:13,916 --> 00:22:17,159
Lugosi 's performance became an original creation of his own ,

407
00:22:17,294 --> 00:22:19,911
blending the Stoker character with other influences

408
00:22:20,005 --> 00:22:23,373
such as stage magicians , continental bedroom villains ,

409
00:22:23,509 --> 00:22:25,500
and Lugosi 's own unique personality .

410
00:22:26,178 --> 00:22:30,843
The actor remained justifiably proud that his Dracula was not Stoker 's ,

411
00:22:31,016 --> 00:22:33,553
but a product of his own talents and perceptions .

412
00:22:33,811 --> 00:22:37,896
And it was Lugosi 's Dracula that lasted throughout the twentieth century

413
00:22:38,190 --> 00:22:40,240
as the most memorable vision of the role .

414
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:44,321
After a two-week tryout in New Haven , Hartford , and other neighboring cities

415
00:22:44,405 --> 00:22:46,521
in late September 1927 ,

416
00:22:46,615 --> 00:22:50,108
Liveright 's Dracula opened on Broadway on October 5th .

417
00:22:50,703 --> 00:22:53,991
As in London , actors in the audience portrayed fainting patrons

418
00:22:54,081 --> 00:22:56,914
at the key moments of horror , and were attended by nurses

419
00:22:57,001 --> 00:23:01,165
who were strategically placed in the lobby for the notice of the arriving audience .

420
00:23:01,755 --> 00:23:04,964
By combining theater with something like carnival fakery ,

421
00:23:05,050 --> 00:23:08,750
Liveright provided an exciting evening , much like the French Grand Guignol .

422
00:23:09,179 --> 00:23:10,294
Before the last act ,

423
00:23:10,389 --> 00:23:14,178
Dracula kissed his female victim passionately on the mouth

424
00:23:14,435 --> 00:23:17,302
before descending to her neck for his feast of blood .

425
00:23:17,980 --> 00:23:21,518
This moment crystallized the sexual attraction of the vampire

426
00:23:21,734 --> 00:23:24,647
and added to the play 's appeal for women in the audience .

427
00:23:24,862 --> 00:23:28,071
Lugosi claimed that most of his fan mail came from women ,

428
00:23:28,157 --> 00:23:30,239
and sexy movie star Clara Bow

429
00:23:30,326 --> 00:23:32,488
became so obsessed with his performance

430
00:23:32,578 --> 00:23:35,115
that she initiated a torrid affair with the actor .

431
00:23:35,831 --> 00:23:38,163
Dracula ran 33 weeks on Broadway

432
00:23:38,250 --> 00:23:41,584
followed by two simultaneous American touring companies ,

433
00:23:41,712 --> 00:23:44,462
one starring Lugosi , and the other with Raymond Huntley .

434
00:23:44,965 --> 00:23:47,252
It earned $ 2 million in America alone ,

435
00:23:47,343 --> 00:23:50,543
while Deane 's version toured England in three separate companies ,

436
00:23:50,721 --> 00:23:54,071
sometimes shortening the play to cram two performances in one night .

437
00:23:54,683 --> 00:23:57,846
Despite Dracula being his most lucrative theatrical endeavor

438
00:23:57,937 --> 00:24:01,726
in a career that included producing The Firebrand in 1924 ,

439
00:24:01,815 --> 00:24:06,901
Hamlet in Modern Dress in 1925 and An American Tragedy in 1926 ,

440
00:24:06,987 --> 00:24:11,606
Horace Liveright foolishly signed away his movie rights to the vampire play .

441
00:24:11,700 --> 00:24:15,245
When Universal bought the rights from Mrs. Stoker , Deane , and Balderston ,

442
00:24:15,329 --> 00:24:16,785
Liveright threatened to sue

443
00:24:16,872 --> 00:24:20,661
on the basis that their film would unfairly compete with his stage version .

444
00:24:21,251 --> 00:24:24,539
Universal paid Liveright off to avoid a nuisance litigation .

445
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:27,963
Nevertheless , Liveright eventually lost his stage rights

446
00:24:28,050 --> 00:24:29,550
over non-payment of royalties .

447
00:24:30,177 --> 00:24:32,259
The stock market crash ruined Liveright ,

448
00:24:32,346 --> 00:24:35,429
and he died alcoholic and broke in 1933 .

449
00:24:36,392 --> 00:24:38,679
Universal already had two movie treatments

450
00:24:38,769 --> 00:24:41,938
written for Dracula by the time they announced Browning as director ,

451
00:24:42,022 --> 00:24:45,515
one by Fritz Stephani and the other by Louis Stevens .

452
00:24:45,651 --> 00:24:48,153
Stephani attempted to combine the novel in the play ,

453
00:24:48,237 --> 00:24:51,605
including showing the plane described by Deane and Balderston

454
00:24:51,699 --> 00:24:55,067
that transports Dracula from Transylvania to London .

455
00:24:55,160 --> 00:24:58,528
Stephani describes the black plane with bat-like wings ,

456
00:24:58,622 --> 00:25:03,287
presumably belonging to Dracula himself and built to his specifications .

457
00:25:03,377 --> 00:25:06,039
Stephani also created the memorable moment

458
00:25:06,130 --> 00:25:10,044
in which the guest to Dracula 's castle sees his driverless carriage

459
00:25:10,134 --> 00:25:13,877
led by a bat flapping before the charging horses .

460
00:25:13,971 --> 00:25:16,679
Novelist Louis Bromfield tackled the adaptation

461
00:25:16,765 --> 00:25:19,678
immediately after Universal named Browning as director .

462
00:25:19,768 --> 00:25:22,020
Richard Schayer of the Universal story department

463
00:25:22,104 --> 00:25:25,938
brought Bromfield 's novel , The Strange Case of Annie Spragg ,

464
00:25:26,025 --> 00:25:28,232
to Laemmle Jr . 's attention .

465
00:25:28,318 --> 00:25:30,111
New Republic reviewed it as a , quote ,

466
00:25:30,195 --> 00:25:32,687
combination of sinister religious influences

467
00:25:32,781 --> 00:25:35,398
with intense dramatic situations , unquote .

468
00:25:35,492 --> 00:25:38,536
This description seemed to mirror the desired effect of Dracula ,

469
00:25:38,620 --> 00:25:40,406
so Bromfield got the job .

470
00:25:40,956 --> 00:25:43,539
In Bromfield 's treatment of August 7 , 1930 ,

471
00:25:43,625 --> 00:25:48,290
John Harker travels by sleigh through a snowstorm to Dracula 's castle .

472
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:52,465
During the trip , piloted by a footman who resembles his host , Count Dracula ,

473
00:25:52,551 --> 00:25:56,545
Harker trains his revolver on a pack of wolves that chase the sleigh .

474
00:25:56,930 --> 00:25:59,843
Bromfield describes Dracula more in agreement with Stoker

475
00:25:59,933 --> 00:26:01,434
than with Deane and Balderston .

476
00:26:01,518 --> 00:26:06,228
Quote , a tall man dressed in musty and unpressed trousers and morning coat .

477
00:26:06,315 --> 00:26:09,478
He has a very pale face , with drooping white mustache ,

478
00:26:09,610 --> 00:26:11,601
and long , unkempt white hair .

479
00:26:11,695 --> 00:26:13,488
Yet he has the distinction of bearing .

480
00:26:13,572 --> 00:26:16,860
He wears also a long black cape , which floats about him

481
00:26:16,950 --> 00:26:19,408
as he moves forward to greet Harker , unquote .

482
00:26:19,536 --> 00:26:21,705
The lasting innovation by Deane and Balderston

483
00:26:21,789 --> 00:26:25,123
was to transform Dracula into a social character

484
00:26:25,209 --> 00:26:29,123
who could interact with his victims before being revealed as a vampire .

485
00:26:29,213 --> 00:26:33,377
To do this , Dracula needed to be attractive , ironic and mysterious ,

486
00:26:33,467 --> 00:26:37,506
and not the repulsive , almost monstrous creature envisioned by Stoker .

487
00:26:37,638 --> 00:26:41,927
The novel 's Dracula remains mostly out of sight once he reaches England .

488
00:26:42,017 --> 00:26:43,977
This wouldn 't work on stage , and it would probably

489
00:26:44,061 --> 00:26:45,861
be far less effective on the screen .

490
00:26:46,814 --> 00:26:49,556
Bromfield tries to have it both ways in his treatment ,

491
00:26:49,650 --> 00:26:52,392
giving the now young Dracula an alias ,

492
00:26:52,486 --> 00:26:55,194
quote , Count de Ville , a handsome , youthful foreigner ,

493
00:26:55,280 --> 00:26:57,988
Hungarian in origin , physically attractive .

494
00:26:58,075 --> 00:26:59,736
He has beautiful , hypnotic eyes ,

495
00:26:59,827 --> 00:27:02,285
which are like the eyes of Dracula , unquote .

496
00:27:02,371 --> 00:27:05,739
Bromfield even allows this disguised Dracula to play bridge

497
00:27:05,833 --> 00:27:08,325
with a fat , wealthy comedy relief widow .

498
00:27:08,710 --> 00:27:10,200
She reveals him as a vampire

499
00:27:10,295 --> 00:27:12,172
when he fails to reflect in her makeup mirror .

500
00:27:12,256 --> 00:27:15,749
Surely not as dramatic as having Harker notice it as in the book ,

501
00:27:15,843 --> 00:27:18,460
or having Van Helsing see it as in the play .

502
00:27:18,762 --> 00:27:21,629
Brownfield 's treatment was dated August 7 .

503
00:27:21,723 --> 00:27:24,267
The same week that Bromfield submitted his treatment ,

504
00:27:24,351 --> 00:27:28,345
Lon Chaney returned to California after learning of his lung cancer ,

505
00:27:28,438 --> 00:27:30,725
and went to his mountain cabin to rest .

506
00:27:30,816 --> 00:27:32,025
He returned to Hollywood ,

507
00:27:32,109 --> 00:27:34,817
but on August the 20th , was rushed to the hospital .

508
00:27:34,903 --> 00:27:38,066
On August the 25th , MGM announced to the press

509
00:27:38,157 --> 00:27:41,534
that , quote , they were pleased with the reported progress of the actor

510
00:27:41,618 --> 00:27:45,236
and declared their belief in his ultimate recovery , unquote .

511
00:27:45,789 --> 00:27:49,248
The next day , Chaney died alone in St. Vincent 's Hospital

512
00:27:49,334 --> 00:27:52,747
from carcinoma of right lower and slight of upper bronchus ,

513
00:27:52,838 --> 00:27:56,251
with pulmonary hemorrhage , not TB , as a contributing factor .

514
00:27:56,383 --> 00:27:57,919
He was 47 years old .

515
00:27:59,178 --> 00:28:02,091
Laemmle Jr . , saddened by the death of his star , pressed on

516
00:28:02,181 --> 00:28:05,431
even as the stock market collapse seriously threatened his studio .

517
00:28:05,767 --> 00:28:07,477
Proving his dedication to the project ,

518
00:28:07,561 --> 00:28:11,646
he announced that almost $ 400,000 would be spent producing Dracula

519
00:28:11,732 --> 00:28:15,082
at a time when half of that amounted to an " A " budget at Universal .

520
00:28:15,777 --> 00:28:19,236
The producer assigned the first draft screenplay to Dudley Murphy .

521
00:28:19,323 --> 00:28:22,065
The son of a Harvard professor , educated at MIT ,

522
00:28:22,159 --> 00:28:24,571
he was a journalist and assistant drama editor

523
00:28:24,661 --> 00:28:28,746
before writing and directing a series of one-reel films in the early ' 20s .

524
00:28:29,291 --> 00:28:31,658
In 1924 , he produced and co-directed

525
00:28:31,752 --> 00:28:36,212
Fernand Léger 's experimental French classic , Ballet mécanique .

526
00:28:36,298 --> 00:28:40,212
Returning to the United States , he continued to write and direct features .

527
00:28:40,302 --> 00:28:43,761
Murphy correctly realized that the lunatic Renfield

528
00:28:43,847 --> 00:28:47,181
was a more interesting character than Mina 's fiancé , Harker .

529
00:28:47,309 --> 00:28:50,017
The writer made the structurally satisfying change

530
00:28:50,103 --> 00:28:53,812
of having Renfield instead of Harker travel to Transylvania

531
00:28:53,899 --> 00:28:55,810
to sell Carfax to Dracula .

532
00:28:56,151 --> 00:28:59,863
In that way , Renfield 's alliance with the vampire could be better motivated ,

533
00:28:59,947 --> 00:29:01,938
and the awkward return of Harker

534
00:29:02,032 --> 00:29:05,382
from his terrifying adventure at Castle Dracula could be eliminated .

535
00:29:05,786 --> 00:29:09,905
This change also increased the horror of Renfield 's fly-eating madness

536
00:29:09,998 --> 00:29:12,298
because he 's first seen as sane and vulnerable .

537
00:29:13,252 --> 00:29:16,370
The other major structural change eliminated much of the action

538
00:29:16,463 --> 00:29:18,213
of the last part of Stoker 's novel .

539
00:29:18,465 --> 00:29:22,880
Every treatment , including Bromfield 's , had followed the same three act division .

540
00:29:22,970 --> 00:29:26,008
The beginning in Transylvania as created by Stoker ,

541
00:29:26,098 --> 00:29:27,634
the middle section in England ,

542
00:29:27,724 --> 00:29:30,432
heavily influenced by Deane and Balderston 's play ,

543
00:29:30,560 --> 00:29:34,303
and the finale , returning to Stoker for the chase back to Transylvania .

544
00:29:35,023 --> 00:29:37,560
Murphy drastically shortened the last act

545
00:29:37,651 --> 00:29:40,564
by having the chase end next door at Carfax .

546
00:29:40,696 --> 00:29:45,315
He did increase the immediate threat by having Dracula carry Mina to Carfax

547
00:29:45,409 --> 00:29:47,867
to bury her until the heroes have died ,

548
00:29:47,953 --> 00:29:50,945
giving him free rein to do with Mina as he chooses .

549
00:29:51,540 --> 00:29:53,747
This doesn 't occur in the play or novel .

550
00:29:54,209 --> 00:29:55,449
In Stoker 's book ,

551
00:29:55,544 --> 00:29:58,878
Mina accompanies the heroes on the chase back to Dracula 's castle ,

552
00:29:59,006 --> 00:30:01,794
using her telepathic connection with the vampire

553
00:30:01,883 --> 00:30:03,840
to guide Van Helsing and the others .

554
00:30:03,927 --> 00:30:06,429
Murphy 's change made the ending far more suspenseful

555
00:30:06,513 --> 00:30:08,003
in the classic Griffith mode ,

556
00:30:08,098 --> 00:30:11,386
with the rescuers hurrying to save the heroine at the last moment .

557
00:30:11,476 --> 00:30:15,811
But it sacrificed the novel 's scope and action , and such evocative scenes

558
00:30:15,897 --> 00:30:18,764
as Dracula 's three brides attempting to seduce Mina

559
00:30:18,859 --> 00:30:21,351
while Van Helsing guards her from their evil .

560
00:30:21,737 --> 00:30:24,149
Murphy keeps the story more intimate .

561
00:30:24,239 --> 00:30:27,825
He eliminates physical movements , such as the running gun battle with Gypsies ,

562
00:30:27,909 --> 00:30:31,402
in favor of a consistently morbid and serious mood .

563
00:30:31,997 --> 00:30:35,160
Murphy reinstated Dracula 's journey by ship to England

564
00:30:35,250 --> 00:30:38,294
that had been modernized into an overnight plane flight by Deane

565
00:30:38,378 --> 00:30:40,745
and ignored by previous screen adapters .

566
00:30:41,173 --> 00:30:43,790
He subtly shows Dracula 's attack on the seamen

567
00:30:43,884 --> 00:30:45,420
in a series of reaction shots ,

568
00:30:45,552 --> 00:30:49,097
and by revealing the dead captain tied to the wheel once the ship reaches port ,

569
00:30:49,181 --> 00:30:50,216
as in the novel .

570
00:30:50,766 --> 00:30:52,723
The most shocking scene in Stoker

571
00:30:52,809 --> 00:30:55,301
is the staking of the British vampire , Lucy .

572
00:30:55,395 --> 00:30:58,183
Of course , nothing remotely like Stoker 's description

573
00:30:58,273 --> 00:31:01,311
could be dramatized in American movies in 1930 .

574
00:31:01,401 --> 00:31:05,440
Murphy 's screenplay suggests the action by having off screen sounds ,

575
00:31:05,530 --> 00:31:09,023
followed by Harker staggering , shaken from Lucy 's tomb .

576
00:31:09,117 --> 00:31:11,905
The finished film eliminates the episode entirely ,

577
00:31:11,995 --> 00:31:15,283
probably a no less effective choice , since it couldn 't approximate

578
00:31:15,374 --> 00:31:16,910
the power of Stoker 's scene .

579
00:31:17,334 --> 00:31:19,701
The main loss in toning down the physical horror

580
00:31:19,795 --> 00:31:22,505
occurred when the filmmakers were forced to portray the death

581
00:31:22,589 --> 00:31:24,639
of their ultimate villain , Count Dracula .

582
00:31:25,092 --> 00:31:28,756
Unable to show the blood-weltering demise described in Stoker ,

583
00:31:28,845 --> 00:31:31,337
Murphy called for another off screen groan

584
00:31:31,431 --> 00:31:34,549
as Van Helsing drives a stake into the vampire 's heart .

585
00:31:34,684 --> 00:31:36,102
This was certainly consistent

586
00:31:36,186 --> 00:31:39,599
with the slow , sinister and serious tone of the rest of the script ,

587
00:31:39,689 --> 00:31:42,852
a mood that could easily be broken by physical violence .

588
00:31:42,943 --> 00:31:44,694
But it seemed an anticlimactic death

589
00:31:44,778 --> 00:31:47,428
for such a powerfully evil character as Count Dracula .

590
00:31:48,031 --> 00:31:50,950
On the other hand , Murphy , as well as the previous adapters ,

591
00:31:51,034 --> 00:31:53,150
greatly enhanced Dracula 's character

592
00:31:53,245 --> 00:31:55,705
by supplanting the middle section of Stoker 's novel

593
00:31:55,789 --> 00:31:57,496
with scenes from the play .

594
00:31:57,624 --> 00:32:00,412
This changed Dracula from an evil but absent force

595
00:32:00,502 --> 00:32:02,834
to a very effective onscreen villain .

596
00:32:03,213 --> 00:32:05,329
Murphy breaks up the claustrophobic quality

597
00:32:05,424 --> 00:32:10,009
of the play 's drawing room setting by staging scenes in foggy London streets ,

598
00:32:10,095 --> 00:32:13,929
a massive theater during a classical music concert , in bedrooms ,

599
00:32:14,015 --> 00:32:17,679
on terraces , in Van Helsing 's laboratory as you see here ,

600
00:32:17,769 --> 00:32:19,385
in Renfield 's asylum cell ,

601
00:32:19,479 --> 00:32:22,440
and on the night-shrouded grounds of Dr. Seward 's Sanitarium .

602
00:32:22,524 --> 00:32:26,859
... devour living things in order to sustain his own life .

603
00:32:27,446 --> 00:32:29,062
But , Professor Van Helsing ,

604
00:32:29,406 --> 00:32:32,273
modern medical science does not admit of such a creature .

605
00:32:33,493 --> 00:32:35,825
The vampire 's a pure myth , a superstition .

606
00:32:35,912 --> 00:32:38,199
I may be able to bring you proof

607
00:32:39,416 --> 00:32:42,829
that the superstition of yesterday

608
00:32:43,545 --> 00:32:48,290
can become the scientific reality of today .

609
00:32:48,383 --> 00:32:50,750
Edward Van Sloan , who plays Dr. Van Helsing ,

610
00:32:50,844 --> 00:32:55,463
was born in San Francisco in 1882 and died in 1964 .

611
00:32:55,557 --> 00:32:59,972
He played Professor Van Helsing in the 1927 Broadway production of Dracula

612
00:33:00,061 --> 00:33:03,270
starring Bela Lugosi , and Dracula was his first movie .

613
00:33:03,857 --> 00:33:05,525
He repeated the role of Van Helsing

614
00:33:05,609 --> 00:33:08,977
in Universal 's 1936 sequel , Dracula 's Daughter ,

615
00:33:09,070 --> 00:33:10,154
and played similar parts

616
00:33:10,238 --> 00:33:14,573
in Universal 's Frankenstein in 1931 , and The Mummy in 1932 ,

617
00:33:14,659 --> 00:33:16,195
both starring Boris Karloff

618
00:33:16,870 --> 00:33:19,328
Herbert Bunston , who plays Dr. Seward in the movie

619
00:33:19,414 --> 00:33:21,405
and on Broadway with Bela Lugosi ,

620
00:33:21,500 --> 00:33:25,494
was born in Surrey , England , on April 15 , 1874 ,

621
00:33:25,587 --> 00:33:30,673
and died of a heart attack in Los Angeles on February 27 , 1935 ,

622
00:33:30,759 --> 00:33:32,466
only four years after this movie .

623
00:33:33,220 --> 00:33:36,303
Dwight Frye , who plays Renfield so memorably ,

624
00:33:36,431 --> 00:33:41,346
was born in Denver , Colorado , in 1899 , and died in 1943 .

625
00:33:41,478 --> 00:33:45,312
Frye became typecast after playing Renfield so well in Dracula

626
00:33:45,440 --> 00:33:48,728
and continued to play Renfield-like assistants and madmen

627
00:33:48,818 --> 00:33:53,688
in Frankenstein , The Vampire Bat , Bride of Frankenstein and Dead Men Walk .

628
00:33:53,782 --> 00:33:56,274
He also had small roles in The Invisible Man ,

629
00:33:56,368 --> 00:33:59,611
Ghost of Frankenstein , and Frankenstein Meets the Wolf Man .

630
00:33:59,704 --> 00:34:03,948
He died suddenly of a heart attack on Sunday , November 7 , 1943

631
00:34:04,042 --> 00:34:05,999
while boarding a bus in Los Angeles

632
00:34:06,086 --> 00:34:08,373
after taking his wife and son to the movies .

633
00:34:08,838 --> 00:34:10,465
At the time , during World War II ,

634
00:34:10,549 --> 00:34:13,382
he also worked nights at the Douglas Aircraft plant .

635
00:34:13,552 --> 00:34:16,044
His death certificate listed his occupation as ,

636
00:34:16,137 --> 00:34:18,299
quote , tool designer , unquote .

637
00:34:19,140 --> 00:34:20,380
In the script ,

638
00:34:20,475 --> 00:34:23,394
this first meeting of Van Helsing and Renfield is much longer ,

639
00:34:23,478 --> 00:34:26,311
with Renfield pretending to be calm and in control .

640
00:34:26,856 --> 00:34:29,188
Browning wisely cut it to the essentials ,

641
00:34:29,276 --> 00:34:32,143
using close shots of Dr. Seward to bridge the cuts .

642
00:34:32,696 --> 00:34:35,615
Melford , of course , plays the whole scene in the Spanish version ,

643
00:34:35,699 --> 00:34:38,316
and it bogs the story down badly at this point .

644
00:34:38,702 --> 00:34:42,070
Browning constantly proves his greater experience as a director .

645
00:34:42,539 --> 00:34:45,372
Even though he keeps Dracula slow and somber ,

646
00:34:45,500 --> 00:34:47,582
he never lets the story stop moving .

647
00:34:49,087 --> 00:34:52,796
Charles Albert Browning was born in Louisville , Kentucky

648
00:34:52,882 --> 00:34:54,919
on July 12 , 1880 .

649
00:34:55,176 --> 00:34:58,794
Infatuated with a dancer in a sideshow , he left home at the age of 16

650
00:34:58,888 --> 00:35:02,756
to literally join the circus , or more precisely , a traveling carnival ,

651
00:35:02,892 --> 00:35:05,509
as a roustabout , an occasional barker .

652
00:35:05,604 --> 00:35:07,971
He changed his name to Tod Browning ,

653
00:35:08,064 --> 00:35:11,307
started performing as a clown , then as a contortionist ,

654
00:35:11,401 --> 00:35:13,444
and even allowed himself to be buried alive

655
00:35:13,528 --> 00:35:16,111
for one or two days as the hypnotic living corpse .

656
00:35:16,990 --> 00:35:20,326
He eventually entered vaudeville , where he performed the same acts on stage

657
00:35:20,410 --> 00:35:22,868
as well as doing comedy and song and dance .

658
00:35:22,954 --> 00:35:25,696
He met D.W.Griffith in 1913

659
00:35:25,790 --> 00:35:28,077
and joined his fledgling team of filmmakers

660
00:35:28,168 --> 00:35:31,286
as an actor , stuntman , and assistant director .

661
00:35:31,421 --> 00:35:33,628
After journeying with Griffith to Hollywood ,

662
00:35:33,715 --> 00:35:36,082
he began directing his own two-reelers

663
00:35:36,176 --> 00:35:39,262
such as The Lucky Transfer , The Living Death and The Burned Hand

664
00:35:39,346 --> 00:35:40,836
in 1915 .

665
00:35:41,181 --> 00:35:42,223
Browning 's new income

666
00:35:42,307 --> 00:35:46,096
allowed him to indulge his love of gambling , liquor , and fast cars .

667
00:35:46,186 --> 00:35:51,647
On the foggy night of June 16 , 1915 , Browning drunkenly plowed his roadster

668
00:35:51,733 --> 00:35:54,270
into a flatbed car loaded with street rails ,

669
00:35:54,402 --> 00:35:57,440
killing his passenger , Griffith actor Elmer Booth ,

670
00:35:57,530 --> 00:36:01,819
and severely injuring himself and second passenger George A. Siegmann ,

671
00:36:01,910 --> 00:36:03,742
another member of the Griffith unit .

672
00:36:04,204 --> 00:36:07,367
After a long convalescence , Browning returned to movies ,

673
00:36:07,457 --> 00:36:10,042
writing films such as The Mystery of the Leaping Fish

674
00:36:10,126 --> 00:36:11,366
for other directors .

675
00:36:11,461 --> 00:36:13,077
The plot of that particular work

676
00:36:13,171 --> 00:36:17,381
involves Douglas Fairbanks as private detective Coke Ennyday ,

677
00:36:17,467 --> 00:36:20,801
who shoots cocaine into the palm of his hand periodically

678
00:36:20,887 --> 00:36:24,425
to assist him in solving a case involving opium smugglers .

679
00:36:25,058 --> 00:36:26,423
Ironically or deliberately ,

680
00:36:26,518 --> 00:36:29,476
Griffith cast Browning less than a year after the accident

681
00:36:29,562 --> 00:36:32,475
as the driver who recklessly races Mae Marsh

682
00:36:32,607 --> 00:36:34,233
to save her husband from the gallows

683
00:36:34,317 --> 00:36:36,854
at the conclusion of his masterpiece , Intolerance .

684
00:36:36,945 --> 00:36:38,652
Good taste is timeless .

685
00:36:38,738 --> 00:36:40,649
The following year , in 1917 ,

686
00:36:40,740 --> 00:36:45,610
Browning co-directed his first feature , Jim Bludso , a Civil War drama .

687
00:36:45,704 --> 00:36:50,323
He then moved to Metro Pictures , where he directed The Jury of Fate in 1917 ,

688
00:36:50,458 --> 00:36:54,247
The Legion of Death , Revenge , and The Eyes of Mystery , all in 1918 .

689
00:36:54,879 --> 00:36:56,756
The last was a haunted house melodrama

690
00:36:56,840 --> 00:36:59,390
that would foreshadow his more macabre work to come .

691
00:37:00,009 --> 00:37:03,468
In 1919 , Universal hired Browning to finish Which Woman ?

692
00:37:03,555 --> 00:37:06,217
For ailing director Harry A. Pollard .

693
00:37:06,307 --> 00:37:09,101
Browning stayed at Universal to direct The Wicked Darling ,

694
00:37:09,185 --> 00:37:13,429
a gangster story that initiated his long collaboration with Lon Chaney .

695
00:37:13,523 --> 00:37:15,650
In their first effort together , Chaney was the villain

696
00:37:15,734 --> 00:37:17,941
opposite Universal star Priscilla Dean ,

697
00:37:18,027 --> 00:37:20,735
who played a female jewel thief with a heart of gold .

698
00:37:22,198 --> 00:37:26,317
Please ! Don 't .

699
00:37:36,337 --> 00:37:40,046
Now , there seems to be some controversy about the piece of cardboard

700
00:37:40,133 --> 00:37:43,376
prominently attached to the lamp next to Mina 's bed .

701
00:37:44,012 --> 00:37:46,219
This is not , as it 's sometimes described ,

702
00:37:46,306 --> 00:37:49,799
a flag for keeping light off of an actor 's face .

703
00:37:49,893 --> 00:37:52,430
The cardboard is set dressing .

704
00:37:52,520 --> 00:37:54,397
It 's meant to be a way of keeping the light

705
00:37:54,481 --> 00:37:57,348
out of the character Mina 's face as she sleeps ,

706
00:37:57,442 --> 00:38:00,025
while the little lamp serves as a night light .

707
00:38:00,111 --> 00:38:02,853
That 's why it 's consistently and unashamedly present

708
00:38:02,989 --> 00:38:05,276
in all the scenes in Mina 's bedroom .

709
00:38:05,366 --> 00:38:08,449
To get this shot , a wild wall had to be removed .

710
00:38:08,536 --> 00:38:10,994
The shot is carefully and beautifully lit .

711
00:38:11,080 --> 00:38:14,289
To think that a flag would be left prominently in the foreground

712
00:38:14,375 --> 00:38:17,788
after all that preparation , and not be caught by cameramen ,

713
00:38:17,879 --> 00:38:20,667
director of photography and script supervisor

714
00:38:20,757 --> 00:38:24,466
shows an incredible ignorance of the filmmaking process .

715
00:38:24,552 --> 00:38:26,387
That such a mistake could be made on the set

716
00:38:26,471 --> 00:38:30,760
of an expensive studio film with a stage full of artists and technicians

717
00:38:30,850 --> 00:38:32,557
is impossible to believe .

718
00:38:32,644 --> 00:38:36,262
It would have been caught in dailies or in rough cut , and reshot .

719
00:38:36,356 --> 00:38:41,101
Obviously , it wasn 't seen as a mistake , but as set dressing .

720
00:38:41,194 --> 00:38:42,650
Anyway , back to Browning .

721
00:38:42,737 --> 00:38:45,570
His alcoholism led to his work becoming sporadic ,

722
00:38:45,657 --> 00:38:47,907
though his first epic , The Virgin of Stamboul ,

723
00:38:48,034 --> 00:38:50,742
was released a great success in 1920 .

724
00:38:50,829 --> 00:38:53,571
His next picture reunited him with Chaney ,

725
00:38:53,665 --> 00:38:56,703
who played both a kindly Chinaman and a vicious gangster

726
00:38:56,793 --> 00:38:59,581
in the Priscilla Dean vehicle , Outside the Law .

727
00:38:59,671 --> 00:39:03,414
After a tremendously violent gunfight at the end , Browning used editing

728
00:39:03,508 --> 00:39:06,708
to show that Chinaman killed the gangster , both played by Chaney .

729
00:39:07,136 --> 00:39:11,630
The Chinaman 's character Ah Wing was billed as , quote , Guess Who , unquote .

730
00:39:12,267 --> 00:39:14,351
The film received generally positive reviews

731
00:39:14,435 --> 00:39:16,687
and did extraordinarily well at the box office .

732
00:39:16,771 --> 00:39:19,857
Nevertheless , Universal no longer considered Browning dependable

733
00:39:19,941 --> 00:39:21,102
due to his drinking .

734
00:39:21,192 --> 00:39:23,319
His last film for the company , The White Tiger ,

735
00:39:23,403 --> 00:39:25,064
sat on the shelf for over a year ,

736
00:39:25,154 --> 00:39:27,823
unreleasable in the form in which Browning delivered it .

737
00:39:27,907 --> 00:39:30,117
The director 's alcoholism cost him the assignment

738
00:39:30,201 --> 00:39:33,193
of Universal 's biggest picture , The Hunchback of Notre Dame ,

739
00:39:33,288 --> 00:39:37,373
even after the studio trade publication had announced him as its director .

740
00:39:37,709 --> 00:39:40,252
" I made an ass of myself , " Browning later admitted .

741
00:39:40,336 --> 00:39:43,704
He managed to secure a one-picture contract with Goldwyn

742
00:39:43,882 --> 00:39:47,546
for The Day of Faith , which received scathing reviews and flopped .

743
00:39:47,635 --> 00:39:49,967
Finally taking stock of his failing fortunes ,

744
00:39:50,054 --> 00:39:52,216
Browning stopped drinking for good .

745
00:39:52,307 --> 00:39:55,049
In 1925 , he approached Irving Thalberg

746
00:39:55,143 --> 00:39:57,635
at the newly formed Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer Studios

747
00:39:57,729 --> 00:40:00,391
to sell him on a film of Aaron " Tod " Robbins '

748
00:40:00,523 --> 00:40:04,983
strange bestselling novel of 1917 , The Unholy Three .

749
00:40:05,445 --> 00:40:08,904
Thalberg saw the potential of this story as a Lon Chaney vehicle ,

750
00:40:08,990 --> 00:40:13,359
bought the rights , and warily offered Browning a one-picture contract .

751
00:40:13,453 --> 00:40:16,616
The director created a stunningly original crime picture

752
00:40:16,706 --> 00:40:19,664
that proved a critical and box office sensation .

753
00:40:19,751 --> 00:40:24,166
It generated a spectacular profit and instantly reestablished Browning

754
00:40:24,255 --> 00:40:26,542
as a commercially viable director .

755
00:40:26,633 --> 00:40:29,421
The studio put Browning under a multi-picture contract

756
00:40:29,510 --> 00:40:32,047
and began the Browning-Chaney association

757
00:40:32,138 --> 00:40:35,597
that would produce some of the best films in both men 's careers .

758
00:40:35,683 --> 00:40:40,177
The Blackbird , The Road to Mandalay , The Unknown , a macabre masterpiece ,

759
00:40:40,688 --> 00:40:43,555
London After Midnight , the first American vampire movie ,

760
00:40:44,067 --> 00:40:48,186
The Big City , West of Zanzibar , another disturbing masterpiece ,

761
00:40:48,321 --> 00:40:50,358
and Where East Is East .

762
00:40:50,490 --> 00:40:54,484
Their next production would have been Dracula , but fate proved otherwise .

763
00:40:57,455 --> 00:41:00,413
But I think Miss Mina should go to her room at once .

764
00:41:00,500 --> 00:41:01,542
Professor Van Helsing ...

765
00:41:01,626 --> 00:41:04,253
Actually , Bela Lugosi may have been Browning 's first choice

766
00:41:04,337 --> 00:41:07,045
for the part of Dracula after the death of Chaney ,

767
00:41:07,131 --> 00:41:08,872
but he was not the studio 's .

768
00:41:09,050 --> 00:41:13,169
Since Lugosi was not well known in movies , Universal considered Ian Keith ,

769
00:41:13,262 --> 00:41:16,471
a dark , handsome actor who was known to have a drinking problem .

770
00:41:16,557 --> 00:41:20,016
Also considered was the respected stage actor William Courtenay ,

771
00:41:20,103 --> 00:41:23,095
who had played a magician on stage in The Spider .

772
00:41:23,189 --> 00:41:27,148
On the B-list was John Wray , Chester Morris , and Paul Muni .

773
00:41:27,235 --> 00:41:30,070
Mrs. Stoker and the playwrights , negotiating through their agent ,

774
00:41:30,154 --> 00:41:32,896
initially favored Joseph Schildkraut .

775
00:41:33,282 --> 00:41:35,114
Perhaps at Tod Browning 's prompting ,

776
00:41:35,201 --> 00:41:39,115
Lugosi got involved in the negotiations for rights with Florence Stoker ,

777
00:41:39,205 --> 00:41:42,197
writing her to help bring down her asking price .

778
00:41:42,291 --> 00:41:44,658
He wired her New York agent , Harold Freedman ,

779
00:41:44,752 --> 00:41:48,495
on April 8 , 1930 , before his official casting .

780
00:41:48,589 --> 00:41:52,002
The wire said , " Have excellent reputable director

781
00:41:52,093 --> 00:41:55,882
" with the biggest studio here willing to buy and do Dracula

782
00:41:55,972 --> 00:42:00,933
" with me , a star , " and asked Friedman to quote his lowest price quick .

783
00:42:01,436 --> 00:42:04,394
On June 25th , Lugosi wired Freedman ,

784
00:42:04,480 --> 00:42:06,687
" Spent many months to promote Dracula , "

785
00:42:06,774 --> 00:42:09,812
" spent many cables with London to bring down price . "

786
00:42:09,902 --> 00:42:12,234
" Will you please express opinion to Universal "

787
00:42:12,321 --> 00:42:16,406
" for me being the logical choice to be cast for Dracula ? "

788
00:42:16,492 --> 00:42:19,109
On July 8th , Lugosi wrote to Friedman .

789
00:42:19,203 --> 00:42:23,242
" I have your letter of June 26 in response to my wire

790
00:42:23,458 --> 00:42:26,621
" and wish to thank you very much for your kind effort

791
00:42:26,711 --> 00:42:30,061
" and suggesting that I played the part in Dracula when it is filmed .

792
00:42:30,506 --> 00:42:35,797
" I 'm sure the success of this enterprise will be largely due to your endeavors , "

793
00:42:35,928 --> 00:42:37,919
" which I very much appreciate . "

794
00:42:38,014 --> 00:42:41,132
" Hoping we may have further business interests together , "

795
00:42:41,225 --> 00:42:45,935
" and thanking you again , I remain , yours very truly , Bela Lugosi . "

796
00:42:46,898 --> 00:42:48,684
As early as July 2nd ,

797
00:42:48,775 --> 00:42:51,767
Browning said in the Los Angeles Examiner that he was for ,

798
00:42:51,861 --> 00:42:56,150
quote , getting a stranger from Europe , and not giving his name , unquote .

799
00:42:56,240 --> 00:42:58,197
That stranger , of course , was Lugosi .

800
00:42:58,326 --> 00:43:01,864
And due to Browning , Lugosi indeed was cast .

801
00:43:01,954 --> 00:43:07,620
So it 's to Tod Browning that we owe the iconic portrayal of Dracula by Lugosi ,

802
00:43:07,710 --> 00:43:10,710
despite all of the opposition he encountered from the studio .

803
00:43:11,422 --> 00:43:14,380
Lugosi had this to say about his most famous role .

804
00:43:15,134 --> 00:43:19,753
" After I had been in the play for a month , I began to take stock of myself ,

805
00:43:19,847 --> 00:43:22,305
" and I realized that for my own well-being ,

806
00:43:22,391 --> 00:43:25,811
" I should make some attempt to conserve my mental and physical strength

807
00:43:25,895 --> 00:43:30,435
" to throw myself with less fervor into the depiction of the role .

808
00:43:30,525 --> 00:43:33,563
" If I could go through the play somewhat mechanically ,

809
00:43:33,736 --> 00:43:35,977
" somewhat more placidly within myself ,

810
00:43:36,072 --> 00:43:39,450
" there would be no lessening of the effect of my performance on the audience ,

811
00:43:39,534 --> 00:43:42,868
" but a decided lessening of the effect on my own nervous system .

812
00:43:43,579 --> 00:43:44,910
" I could not do it .

813
00:43:44,997 --> 00:43:49,662
" The role seemed to demand that I keep myself worked up to a fever pitch ,

814
00:43:49,752 --> 00:43:51,413
" and so I sat in my dressing room

815
00:43:51,546 --> 00:43:54,834
" and took on as nearly as possible , the actual attributes

816
00:43:54,924 --> 00:43:57,291
" of the horrible vampire Dracula .

817
00:43:57,385 --> 00:44:00,343
" And during those two years , I did not speak a word

818
00:44:00,429 --> 00:44:04,593
" to any person behind the scenes during the progress of the play .

819
00:44:04,684 --> 00:44:09,770
" And since everyone knew of the strain I was laboring under , no one spoke to me .

820
00:44:09,856 --> 00:44:13,941
" I was under a veritable spell , which I dared not break . "

821
00:44:14,026 --> 00:44:17,940
" If I stepped out of my character for even a moment , the seething menace "

822
00:44:18,030 --> 00:44:22,194
" of the terrible Count Dracula was gone from the characterization , "

823
00:44:22,285 --> 00:44:25,403
" and my hold on the audience lost its force . "

824
00:44:26,414 --> 00:44:30,157
Tod Browning taught Lugosi to modify his technique for the film .

825
00:44:30,251 --> 00:44:34,836
" Lugosi said , " In the theater , I was playing not only to the spectators

826
00:44:34,922 --> 00:44:38,665
" in the front rows , but also to those in the last rows of the gallery .

827
00:44:38,885 --> 00:44:41,968
" A certain exaggeration in everything I did ,

828
00:44:42,054 --> 00:44:44,386
" not only in the tone or pitch of my voice ,

829
00:44:44,473 --> 00:44:46,680
" but in the changes of facial expression ,

830
00:44:46,767 --> 00:44:50,761
" which accompanied various lines or situations , was necessary .

831
00:44:50,897 --> 00:44:53,685
" I took it big , as the saying is .

832
00:44:53,774 --> 00:44:58,519
" But for the screen , in which the actor 's distance from every member of the audience

833
00:44:58,613 --> 00:45:01,776
" is equal only to the distance from the lens of the camera ,

834
00:45:01,866 --> 00:45:06,076
" I have found a great deal of repression was absolutely necessary .

835
00:45:06,162 --> 00:45:09,655
" Tod Browning has continually had to hold me down .

836
00:45:09,749 --> 00:45:12,793
" In other screen roles , I did not seem to have this difficulty ,

837
00:45:12,877 --> 00:45:16,620
" but I have played Dracula a thousand times on the stage , "

838
00:45:16,714 --> 00:45:20,673
" and in this one role , I find that I have become thoroughly settled "

839
00:45:20,760 --> 00:45:23,593
" in the technique of the stage and not of the screen . "

840
00:45:23,679 --> 00:45:26,671
" But thanks to my director , I am unlearning fast . "

841
00:45:28,100 --> 00:45:29,090
... your police .

842
00:45:31,562 --> 00:45:34,554
The strength of the vampire is that people will ...

843
00:45:34,649 --> 00:45:37,186
Lew Ayres , who starred in Universal 's last hit ,

844
00:45:37,276 --> 00:45:40,860
All Quiet on the Western Front , was originally cast as Jonathan Harker ,

845
00:45:40,947 --> 00:45:41,982
but was dropped .

846
00:45:42,073 --> 00:45:44,575
He later said that the part that really interested him

847
00:45:44,659 --> 00:45:47,559
was , quote , the guy who ate the flies and spiders , unquote .

848
00:45:48,162 --> 00:45:53,453
David Manners , who plays Jonathan Harker , was born Rauff de Ryther Duan Acklom

849
00:45:53,542 --> 00:45:57,536
on April 30th , 1900 , in Halifax , Nova Scotia .

850
00:45:57,630 --> 00:46:01,624
His first movie was Journey 's End in 1930 , directed by James Whale ,

851
00:46:01,717 --> 00:46:04,584
who would go on to direct Frankenstein in 1931 .

852
00:46:05,179 --> 00:46:07,598
After Dracula , Manners played the hero in The Mummy

853
00:46:07,682 --> 00:46:11,300
and The Black Cat and Edwin Drood in The Mystery of Edwin Drood .

854
00:46:11,394 --> 00:46:14,978
He hated making movies and retired from the screen in the mid ' 30s ,

855
00:46:15,064 --> 00:46:17,681
but occasionally appeared on stage and wrote novels .

856
00:46:18,359 --> 00:46:20,652
David Manners died in Santa Barbara , California ,

857
00:46:20,736 --> 00:46:23,068
on December 23rd , 1998 .

858
00:46:23,781 --> 00:46:27,775
Helen Chandler , who plays Mina , was born on February 1st , 1906 ,

859
00:46:27,868 --> 00:46:29,984
in Charleston , South Carolina .

860
00:46:30,079 --> 00:46:32,662
She started acting on stage at the age of eight .

861
00:46:32,748 --> 00:46:34,374
She played one of the boy princes

862
00:46:34,458 --> 00:46:38,122
opposite John Barrymore 's legendary Richard III on Broadway .

863
00:46:38,212 --> 00:46:41,215
She also played Ophelia in the controversial modern-dress Hamlet

864
00:46:41,299 --> 00:46:44,792
produced by Horace Liveright , the producer of Dracula on Broadway .

865
00:46:44,885 --> 00:46:48,219
She began acting in silent movies in 1927 .

866
00:46:48,306 --> 00:46:49,431
The year before Dracula ,

867
00:46:49,515 --> 00:46:53,165
she appeared in the movie adaptation of the strange fantasy Outward Bound .

868
00:46:53,602 --> 00:46:56,060
Tod Browning saw her opening night performance

869
00:46:56,147 --> 00:46:59,731
in the stage mystery Silent House , and cast her as Mina .

870
00:46:59,817 --> 00:47:02,605
Chandler told an interviewer in 1932 ,

871
00:47:02,695 --> 00:47:05,447
" In Dracula , I played one of those bewildered little girls "

872
00:47:05,531 --> 00:47:09,650
" who go around pale , hollow-eyed , and anguished , wondering about things . "

873
00:47:09,744 --> 00:47:13,032
Among her several husbands were screenwriter Cyril Hume

874
00:47:13,122 --> 00:47:16,831
and actor Bramwell Fletcher , so memorable as the young archaeologist

875
00:47:16,917 --> 00:47:19,909
who accidentally brings Karloff to life in The Mummy .

876
00:47:20,004 --> 00:47:22,214
Suffering from alcoholism , she retired from the screen

877
00:47:22,298 --> 00:47:25,131
in the mid ' 30s and died in 1965 .

878
00:47:25,217 --> 00:47:27,667
She was cremated and her ashes were never claimed .

879
00:47:28,346 --> 00:47:32,715
Frances Dade , who plays Lucy , was born on February 14th , 1910 ,

880
00:47:32,808 --> 00:47:37,473
in Philadelphia , and died there on January 21st , 1968 .

881
00:47:37,563 --> 00:47:40,190
She starred in the hit stage play , Gentlemen Prefer Blondes ,

882
00:47:40,274 --> 00:47:44,142
and made her movie debut opposite Ronald Colman in Raffles .

883
00:47:44,779 --> 00:47:47,066
Her grandmother , Mrs. Clifford Pemberton ,

884
00:47:47,198 --> 00:47:50,316
had been the model for the profile on the Liberty Dollar .

885
00:47:50,409 --> 00:47:55,529
Her last movie was Big Town in 1932 , the year following the release of Dracula .

886
00:47:55,623 --> 00:47:57,541
As the casting firmed up , Browning worked

887
00:47:57,625 --> 00:48:01,459
with Universal writer Garrett Fort on polishing Murphy 's script .

888
00:48:01,545 --> 00:48:06,506
He resisted several changes in the script requested by no less than Carl Laemmle Jr .

889
00:48:06,592 --> 00:48:09,835
For example , Laemmle wanted to eliminate Dracula 's line ,

890
00:48:09,970 --> 00:48:13,804
" To die , to be really dead , that must be glorious . "

891
00:48:13,891 --> 00:48:17,885
" There are far worse things awaiting man than death . "

892
00:48:17,978 --> 00:48:19,434
Laemmle also insisted that ,

893
00:48:19,522 --> 00:48:22,522
quote , Dracula should only go for women and not men , unquote .

894
00:48:22,650 --> 00:48:26,188
But Browning retained Dracula 's destruction of the ship 's crew

895
00:48:26,278 --> 00:48:28,645
and even had the vampire drink from Renfield

896
00:48:28,739 --> 00:48:31,982
instead of leaving it to his brides , as called for in the script .

897
00:48:32,076 --> 00:48:35,410
Browning also defied his boss by keeping the shot of Dracula

898
00:48:35,496 --> 00:48:38,830
walking through the fog after his attack on the flower girl .

899
00:48:39,250 --> 00:48:42,163
In the earlier September 8 , 1930 draft of the script ,

900
00:48:42,253 --> 00:48:44,540
after Dracula attacks the flower girl ,

901
00:48:44,630 --> 00:48:47,338
he sees Dr. Seward and his party getting into a cab

902
00:48:47,425 --> 00:48:50,338
and follows them back to Dr. Seward 's Sanitarium .

903
00:48:51,303 --> 00:48:53,260
In that draft , the concert hall scene

904
00:48:53,347 --> 00:48:57,056
takes place in Dr. Seward 's living room , with Mina playing the piano .

905
00:48:57,143 --> 00:49:00,681
Browning and Fort wisely took advantage of the standing auditorium set

906
00:49:00,771 --> 00:49:03,821
from The Phantom of the Opera to give the scene greater scope .

907
00:49:03,941 --> 00:49:06,360
The final script gave credit to both Browning and Fort

908
00:49:06,444 --> 00:49:08,526
for adaptation and dialogue .

909
00:49:08,612 --> 00:49:11,024
In the end , both Browning 's and Murphy 's names

910
00:49:11,115 --> 00:49:13,492
would be eliminated from the credits for screenplay .

911
00:49:13,576 --> 00:49:15,613
Only Garrett Fort is given billing .

912
00:49:22,543 --> 00:49:24,910
Browning finally started shooting Dracula

913
00:49:25,004 --> 00:49:29,714
on September 29th , 1930 , and wrapped on November 15th .

914
00:49:29,800 --> 00:49:34,135
Additional scenes and retakes were filmed on December 13th , 1930 ,

915
00:49:34,221 --> 00:49:36,633
and January 2nd , 1931 .

916
00:49:37,266 --> 00:49:39,928
After carefully casting and designing the film ,

917
00:49:40,102 --> 00:49:42,935
Browning reportedly kept a low profile on the set .

918
00:49:43,439 --> 00:49:46,557
At least it was never said , as it has been said several times

919
00:49:46,650 --> 00:49:49,108
of another careful planner , Alfred Hitchcock ,

920
00:49:49,195 --> 00:49:53,154
that Browning found filming so boring , he occasionally fell asleep during takes .

921
00:49:53,866 --> 00:49:57,029
Actually , according to Lugosi , Browning had to constantly fight

922
00:49:57,161 --> 00:49:59,653
with the studio to maintain his vision .

923
00:49:59,747 --> 00:50:04,617
" Lugosi remembered , " Everything that Tod Browning wanted to do was queried .

924
00:50:04,710 --> 00:50:08,578
" Couldn 't it be done cheaper ? Would it be just as effective if ? "

925
00:50:08,672 --> 00:50:11,960
" That sort of thing . It was most dispiriting . "

926
00:50:12,051 --> 00:50:14,042
But Browning did it his way .

927
00:50:14,136 --> 00:50:16,503
According to Carl Laemmle Jr . 's private ledger ,

928
00:50:16,597 --> 00:50:22,934
Dracula eventually cost $ 441,984.90 ,

929
00:50:23,020 --> 00:50:26,012
more than $ 85,000 over budget .

930
00:50:26,106 --> 00:50:30,145
Scheduled for 36 days , Browning actually filmed for 42 .

931
00:50:30,236 --> 00:50:34,946
The studio eventually reduced his 84-minute cut to 75 .

932
00:50:35,032 --> 00:50:36,700
The final released version included

933
00:50:36,784 --> 00:50:40,652
the now lost curtain speech by Edward Van Sloan at the end .

934
00:50:40,746 --> 00:50:44,535
That scene was eliminated for the 1938 re-release so as not to offend

935
00:50:44,625 --> 00:50:46,832
religious groups insulted to think that

936
00:50:46,919 --> 00:50:50,297
vampires could really exist when there 's no mention of them in the Bible .

937
00:50:50,381 --> 00:50:52,213
The scene never returned .

938
00:50:52,299 --> 00:50:56,293
Dracula premiered at the prestigious Roxy Theater in New York

939
00:50:56,387 --> 00:50:59,505
on Thursday , February 12th , 1931 ,

940
00:50:59,598 --> 00:51:03,887
so as to avoid an unlucky opening the next day on Friday the 13th .

941
00:51:03,978 --> 00:51:08,267
The theater sold over 50,000 tickets in the first 48 hours .

942
00:51:08,357 --> 00:51:11,145
To give an idea of the initial impact of the film ,

943
00:51:11,235 --> 00:51:16,150
we have the Friday the 13th review in the New York Times by Mordaunt Hall .

944
00:51:16,240 --> 00:51:19,278
He wrote that , Dracula , quote , had the desired effect

945
00:51:19,368 --> 00:51:22,110
upon many in the audience yesterday afternoon .

946
00:51:22,204 --> 00:51:24,866
For there was a general outburst of applause

947
00:51:24,957 --> 00:51:27,324
when Dr. Van Helsing produced a little cross

948
00:51:27,418 --> 00:51:29,079
that caused the dreaded Dracula

949
00:51:29,169 --> 00:51:32,207
to fling his cloak over his head and make himself scarce .

950
00:51:32,298 --> 00:51:33,663
Mr. Browning is fortunate

951
00:51:33,757 --> 00:51:37,170
in having in the lead role , in this eerie work , Bela Lugosi ,

952
00:51:37,261 --> 00:51:39,093
who played the same part on stage

953
00:51:39,179 --> 00:51:42,388
when it was presented here in October , 1927 .

954
00:51:42,474 --> 00:51:45,057
What with Mr. Browning 's imaginative direction

955
00:51:45,144 --> 00:51:47,636
and Mr. Lugosi 's makeup and weird gestures ,

956
00:51:47,730 --> 00:51:50,222
this picture can at least boast of being the best

957
00:51:50,316 --> 00:51:52,808
of the many mystery films , unquote .

958
00:51:52,901 --> 00:51:54,733
The New York Daily News said ,

959
00:51:54,820 --> 00:51:58,859
" It is superbly photographed and presented to audible screen audiences

960
00:51:58,949 --> 00:52:01,816
" at the capable movie direction of Tod Browning ,

961
00:52:01,910 --> 00:52:05,744
" megaphoner of so many mysteries that he knows every trick of the type .

962
00:52:05,831 --> 00:52:09,290
" Carl Laemmle Jr. counts Dracula among his productions for the season ,

963
00:52:09,376 --> 00:52:11,287
" and the boy 's done well again .

964
00:52:11,378 --> 00:52:14,291
" He chose director , cast , and story wisely . "

965
00:52:14,381 --> 00:52:16,622
" Bela Lugosi 's performance as Count Dracula "

966
00:52:16,717 --> 00:52:18,799
" is a repetition of his stage role . "

967
00:52:18,886 --> 00:52:20,547
" He 's simply grand . "

968
00:52:20,638 --> 00:52:23,255
The San Francisco Examiners ' Ada Hanifin

969
00:52:23,349 --> 00:52:26,683
took notice of the film 's , quote , tragic dignity , unquote ,

970
00:52:26,769 --> 00:52:28,430
and said of Tod Browning ,

971
00:52:28,520 --> 00:52:31,387
" His fertile imagination and artistic ingenuity ,

972
00:52:31,482 --> 00:52:36,067
" are seemingly inexhaustible . The photographic treatment is magnificent .

973
00:52:36,153 --> 00:52:40,238
" The fall of light and shadows , dim forms of vaulted castles , "

974
00:52:40,324 --> 00:52:42,861
" the sudden coming on of dank mists , "

975
00:52:42,951 --> 00:52:46,489
" strange lights that illuminate the pallid fiend 's eyes , "

976
00:52:46,580 --> 00:52:49,663
" a well-nigh perfect thing of its kind . "

977
00:52:49,750 --> 00:52:52,538
After a critically lauded trade preview in London ,

978
00:52:52,628 --> 00:52:56,337
The Era called Dracula , quote , gloominess and excelsis .

979
00:52:56,423 --> 00:53:00,166
It is more than possible that its gruesomeness will hit a high spot

980
00:53:00,260 --> 00:53:04,470
among the popular audiences , especially in provincial towns , unquote .

981
00:53:04,890 --> 00:53:06,597
Of Bela Lugosi , Variety said ,

982
00:53:06,684 --> 00:53:09,221
quote , it would be difficult to think of anybody

983
00:53:09,311 --> 00:53:12,019
who could quite match the performance , unquote .

984
00:53:12,106 --> 00:53:16,566
Dracula was huge hit in every city it played , with three-week holdovers ,

985
00:53:16,652 --> 00:53:20,566
early morning performances and even 24-hour screenings .

986
00:53:20,906 --> 00:53:25,195
It grossed $ 700,000 in its first release domestic rentals ,

987
00:53:25,285 --> 00:53:27,822
$ 1.2 million worldwide .

988
00:53:27,913 --> 00:53:31,747
Dracula saved the wobbly finances of Universal and gave the studio

989
00:53:31,834 --> 00:53:34,496
its only profitable year during the Depression .

990
00:53:34,962 --> 00:53:37,954
Not only that , it continued to rake in money for Universal

991
00:53:38,048 --> 00:53:41,916
and its theatrical re-releases during the ' 30s , ' 40s and ' 50s .

992
00:53:42,010 --> 00:53:45,503
Dracula was the biggest commercial success of Tod Browning 's career

993
00:53:45,597 --> 00:53:49,556
and a high point in his many explorations of the strange and morbid .

994
00:53:49,643 --> 00:53:51,429
Browning was one of America 's ,

995
00:53:51,520 --> 00:53:54,979
and indeed the world 's , first auteurs of the horror film .

996
00:53:55,065 --> 00:53:58,148
Unlike James Whale , who would use his brilliant theatrical

997
00:53:58,235 --> 00:53:59,819
and cinematic techniques to bring

998
00:53:59,903 --> 00:54:02,565
Frankenstein and The Invisible Man to the screen ,

999
00:54:02,656 --> 00:54:05,899
Browning was well-versed in horror as entertainment .

1000
00:54:05,993 --> 00:54:09,236
Director Edgar G. Ulmer , another pioneer of terror onscreen

1001
00:54:09,329 --> 00:54:12,447
in such classics as The Black Cat and Bluebeard , said that ,

1002
00:54:12,541 --> 00:54:14,407
quote , Tod Browning was perhaps

1003
00:54:14,501 --> 00:54:18,495
the first one to have seen what were called then fantasy films

1004
00:54:18,589 --> 00:54:21,001
which were being made in Germany and Sweden .

1005
00:54:21,091 --> 00:54:25,210
After the war in Germany , there was a strong E.T.A Hoffman influence .

1006
00:54:25,304 --> 00:54:27,545
Browning knew Wiene 's work in Caligari

1007
00:54:27,639 --> 00:54:30,597
but began to get away from the expressionism of the period

1008
00:54:30,684 --> 00:54:33,676
into a conception which one might call baroque .

1009
00:54:33,771 --> 00:54:37,605
Browning was a man of infinitely wider culture than most people .

1010
00:54:37,691 --> 00:54:41,025
He was widely read , and not only knew Poe by heart ,

1011
00:54:41,111 --> 00:54:43,398
but all of English Gothic literature .

1012
00:54:43,822 --> 00:54:46,860
That being the case , Browning knew Bram Stoker 's influences

1013
00:54:46,950 --> 00:54:48,987
when writing the novel Dracula .

1014
00:54:49,077 --> 00:54:52,911
Dracula was not the first vampire popular in English literature and theater .

1015
00:54:52,998 --> 00:54:55,535
That distinction belongs to Lord Ruthven ,

1016
00:54:55,667 --> 00:54:59,877
the socially acceptable nobleman and bloodsucker created in 1819

1017
00:54:59,963 --> 00:55:02,045
by 23-year-old John Polidori ,

1018
00:55:02,132 --> 00:55:05,625
physician to the great British romantic poet , Lord Byron .

1019
00:55:06,094 --> 00:55:09,883
Polidori revised and enlarged a fragment of a novel written by Byron

1020
00:55:09,973 --> 00:55:13,807
that was created in response to the same challenge to devise a ghost story

1021
00:55:13,894 --> 00:55:16,556
that resulted in Mary Shelley 's Frankenstein .

1022
00:55:16,647 --> 00:55:18,979
So popular was Polidori 's The Vampyre ,

1023
00:55:19,066 --> 00:55:22,066
which actually resembled Lugosi 's Dracula more than Stoker 's ,

1024
00:55:22,152 --> 00:55:26,396
that several theatrical versions were mounted in the mid-19th century .

1025
00:55:26,865 --> 00:55:31,405
Polidori based the personality of his vampire on Lord Byron himself

1026
00:55:31,495 --> 00:55:36,160
Brilliant , arrogant , without conscience , and fatally attractive .

1027
00:55:36,583 --> 00:55:41,453
Like Lugosi 's Dracula , Ruthven preys on the women on London 's society .

1028
00:55:41,547 --> 00:55:43,959
Polidori describes him this way .

1029
00:55:44,258 --> 00:55:46,795
" It happened that in the midst of the dissipations

1030
00:55:46,885 --> 00:55:48,136
" attendant upon a London winter ,

1031
00:55:48,220 --> 00:55:51,633
" there appeared at the various parties of the leaders of the ton ,

1032
00:55:51,723 --> 00:55:56,217
" a nobleman , more remarkable for his singularity than his rank .

1033
00:55:56,311 --> 00:56:01,351
" He gazed upon the mirth around him as if he could not participate therein .

1034
00:56:01,441 --> 00:56:05,730
" Apparently , the light laughter of the fare only attracted his attention

1035
00:56:05,821 --> 00:56:08,404
" that he might by a look , quell it ,

1036
00:56:08,490 --> 00:56:13,075
" and throw fear into those breasts where thoughtlessness reigned .

1037
00:56:13,161 --> 00:56:17,496
" Those who felt the sensation of awe could not explain whence it arose . "

1038
00:56:17,583 --> 00:56:20,371
" Some attributed it to the dead gray eye , "

1039
00:56:20,460 --> 00:56:25,045
" which , fixing upon the object 's face , did not seem to penetrate . "

1040
00:56:25,132 --> 00:56:30,093
" His peculiarities caused him to be invited to every house . "

1041
00:56:30,596 --> 00:56:33,338
This could easily be a description of Lugosi 's impact

1042
00:56:33,432 --> 00:56:36,720
on Dr. Seward 's little group at the theater in Browning 's film .

1043
00:56:36,810 --> 00:56:38,937
Many publishers and those of the reading public

1044
00:56:39,021 --> 00:56:40,932
attributed the vampire to Byron .

1045
00:56:41,023 --> 00:56:44,311
Polidori wanted credit and Byron hated the subject matter .

1046
00:56:44,401 --> 00:56:48,279
Reluctantly , publishers finally admitted that the tale was written by Polidori ,

1047
00:56:48,363 --> 00:56:50,900
but the author 's triumph was short-lived .

1048
00:56:51,033 --> 00:56:54,571
He died two years later of brain damage after a carriage accident .

1049
00:56:55,037 --> 00:56:58,575
Still bitter at his former doctor , Byron started the rumor that

1050
00:56:58,665 --> 00:57:01,532
Polidori committed suicide from shame .

1051
00:57:01,627 --> 00:57:05,040
So popular was the book , that several sequels and plays followed .

1052
00:57:05,130 --> 00:57:09,966
In 1846 , a serialized publication called Varney the Vampire ; or , the Feast of Blood

1053
00:57:10,052 --> 00:57:11,668
by James Malcolm Rymer

1054
00:57:11,762 --> 00:57:14,629
retold Polidori 's 20-page story repeatedly

1055
00:57:14,723 --> 00:57:19,684
over 868 double column pages in increasingly lurid detail .

1056
00:57:20,145 --> 00:57:23,228
In terms of literature , the finest antecedent of Dracula

1057
00:57:23,315 --> 00:57:28,401
was the story Carmilla , written in 1872 by the Dubliner Sheridan Le Fanu .

1058
00:57:28,487 --> 00:57:30,819
He tells of the strange lesbian relationship

1059
00:57:30,906 --> 00:57:33,989
between the 19-year-old narrator and an ancient vampire

1060
00:57:34,076 --> 00:57:36,411
with the appearance of a beautiful teenage girl ,

1061
00:57:36,495 --> 00:57:39,328
the Styrian countess , Carmilla Karnstein .

1062
00:57:39,414 --> 00:57:43,123
Sexual lust and blood lust commingle in the breathless narrative ,

1063
00:57:43,210 --> 00:57:46,202
confusing the line between love and death .

1064
00:57:46,296 --> 00:57:49,584
Countess Karnstein tells her innocent , lovely young victim ,

1065
00:57:49,675 --> 00:57:53,168
" Think me not cruel because I obey the irresistible law "

1066
00:57:53,261 --> 00:57:57,505
" of my strength and weakness . In the rapture of my enormous humiliation , "

1067
00:57:57,599 --> 00:58:03,971
" I live in your warm life , and you shall die , die , sweetly die , into mine . "

1068
00:58:04,314 --> 00:58:05,770
" I cannot help it . "

1069
00:58:05,857 --> 00:58:08,690
Doctor , this time he can do no harm .

1070
00:58:08,777 --> 00:58:11,690
- We are ready for him . - Martin , come with me .

1071
00:58:11,780 --> 00:58:14,824
I 'll show you where we can put Mr. Renfield where he won 't escape again .

1072
00:58:14,908 --> 00:58:18,742
Well , all right , but I have me doubts . Come along , old fly-eater .

1073
00:58:18,829 --> 00:58:20,455
Now you mustn 't get out of it this time .

1074
00:58:20,539 --> 00:58:21,956
I 'm awfully sorry to have to lock you .

1075
00:58:22,040 --> 00:58:23,872
You 've got to stay in your room ...

1076
00:58:25,002 --> 00:58:26,584
Van Helsing !

1077
00:58:27,546 --> 00:58:30,163
Melford 's Spanish version completely blunts

1078
00:58:30,257 --> 00:58:32,498
the impact of this powerful confrontation

1079
00:58:32,592 --> 00:58:34,708
between Dracula and Van Helsing

1080
00:58:34,803 --> 00:58:38,888
by foolishly intercutting it with a scene between Mina and Harker .

1081
00:58:38,974 --> 00:58:42,717
Melford actually destroys the tension of both scenes

1082
00:58:42,811 --> 00:58:45,428
since they contain their conflicts within themselves

1083
00:58:45,522 --> 00:58:47,229
and don 't relate to each other .

1084
00:58:47,315 --> 00:58:50,182
He also causes Dracula to seem stupid

1085
00:58:50,277 --> 00:58:53,565
by having him release his hypnotic power on Van Helsing ,

1086
00:58:53,655 --> 00:58:58,491
and command him to put the cross into a convenient box on a table between them .

1087
00:58:58,577 --> 00:59:01,287
Of course , Van Helsing keeps the cross , tricking Dracula

1088
00:59:01,371 --> 00:59:03,414
and then showing it to him , which drives him away .

1089
00:59:03,498 --> 00:59:05,739
Browning 's version is much more effective .

1090
00:59:05,834 --> 00:59:09,372
With Val Helsing struggling to resist Dracula with all his might ,

1091
00:59:09,463 --> 00:59:13,252
and Dracula arrogantly assuming that Van Helsing is going to threaten him

1092
00:59:13,341 --> 00:59:15,799
with a useless weapon instead of the cross .

1093
00:59:16,303 --> 00:59:19,170
Another example of Browning 's directorial savvy ,

1094
00:59:19,264 --> 00:59:23,383
altering the script on the set for a far more effective scene .

1095
00:59:23,935 --> 00:59:24,970
Let 's watch .

1096
00:59:25,062 --> 00:59:26,644
... through your heart .

1097
00:59:26,855 --> 00:59:27,936
Come here .

1098
00:59:45,290 --> 00:59:46,371
Come

1099
00:59:48,168 --> 00:59:49,249
here .

1100
01:00:15,946 --> 01:00:18,028
Both actors were terrific in this .

1101
01:00:18,907 --> 01:00:20,739
Bela Lugosi is so commanding ,

1102
01:00:20,826 --> 01:00:23,693
you can 't resist him , and yet , Van Helsing does .

1103
01:00:23,870 --> 01:00:27,613
... is strong , Van Helsing .

1104
01:00:33,713 --> 01:00:36,922
More wolf bane ?

1105
01:00:37,300 --> 01:00:40,588
More effective than wolf bane , Count .

1106
01:00:40,679 --> 01:00:42,215
Indeed ?

1107
01:00:44,015 --> 01:00:45,847
Yeah , you can 't do better than that .

1108
01:00:47,269 --> 01:00:50,227
Both Le Fanu and Bram Stoker came from a similar

1109
01:00:50,313 --> 01:00:52,554
Anglo-Irish protestant background .

1110
01:00:52,649 --> 01:00:56,859
Bram Stoker grew up in the seaside suburb of Clontarf , Dublin ,

1111
01:00:56,945 --> 01:00:59,653
and spoke with a thick Irish brogue all his life .

1112
01:00:59,739 --> 01:01:02,902
A civil servant who wrote a 248-page book

1113
01:01:02,993 --> 01:01:05,826
called The Duties of Clerks of Petty Sessions in Ireland ,

1114
01:01:05,912 --> 01:01:07,823
while inspector of Petty Sessions ,

1115
01:01:07,914 --> 01:01:12,704
he reinvented himself when he met the flamboyant actor-manager Henry Irving .

1116
01:01:12,794 --> 01:01:15,912
In 1878 , Stoker became his business manager

1117
01:01:16,006 --> 01:01:18,043
and front of house master of ceremonies

1118
01:01:18,133 --> 01:01:21,376
at his Lyceum Theater off the strand in London .

1119
01:01:21,469 --> 01:01:25,463
As such , he rubbed elbows with the elite of London society and art .

1120
01:01:25,891 --> 01:01:29,634
On March 8th , 1890 , inspired by a vivid dream ,

1121
01:01:29,728 --> 01:01:32,891
42-year-old Stoker wrote on a piece of scrap paper ,

1122
01:01:32,981 --> 01:01:37,066
quote , young man goes out , sees girls .

1123
01:01:37,152 --> 01:01:40,895
One tries to kiss him , not on the lips , but throat .

1124
01:01:40,989 --> 01:01:45,358
Old Count interferes . Rage and fury diabolical .

1125
01:01:45,452 --> 01:01:49,116
This man belongs to me . I want him . Unquote .

1126
01:01:49,497 --> 01:01:51,784
That was the beginning of an artistic journey

1127
01:01:51,875 --> 01:01:56,164
that ended in June , 1897 , with the publication of Dracula .

1128
01:01:56,546 --> 01:02:00,084
Like La Fenu 's Carmilla , Stoker 's Dracula was originally

1129
01:02:00,175 --> 01:02:02,416
to come from Styria , part of Austria .

1130
01:02:02,510 --> 01:02:06,378
But Stoker read The Land Beyond the Forest by Emily Gerard , which stated that ,

1131
01:02:06,473 --> 01:02:11,058
quote , Transylvania might well be termed " The Land of Superstition "

1132
01:02:11,144 --> 01:02:15,388
for nowhere else does this curious , crooked plant of delusion flourish

1133
01:02:15,482 --> 01:02:18,691
as persistently and in such bewildering variety .

1134
01:02:18,777 --> 01:02:22,645
It would almost seem as though the whole species of demons , pixies ,

1135
01:02:22,739 --> 01:02:25,857
witches , and hobgoblins driven from the rest of Europe

1136
01:02:25,951 --> 01:02:30,912
by the wand of science had taken refuge within this mountain rampart , unquote .

1137
01:02:31,539 --> 01:02:35,703
In this catalog she includes , quote , the Vampire or Nosferatu ,

1138
01:02:35,794 --> 01:02:38,035
in whom every Romanian peasant believes

1139
01:02:38,129 --> 01:02:41,838
as firmly as he does in heaven and hell , unquote .

1140
01:02:42,342 --> 01:02:45,835
Stoker never actually visited Transylvania , but researched it

1141
01:02:45,929 --> 01:02:49,429
from books and maps in the circular reading room at the British Museum .

1142
01:02:49,975 --> 01:02:54,390
He found the name for his vampire in a 1492 Bamberg pamphlet

1143
01:02:54,479 --> 01:02:56,436
about a voivode of Wallachia ,

1144
01:02:56,523 --> 01:03:00,983
who died in 1476 , named Vlad Dracula ,

1145
01:03:01,069 --> 01:03:03,731
who was also called " Tepee " or " The Impaler "

1146
01:03:03,822 --> 01:03:08,316
because of the way he executed rival Wallachian boyars and Turkish prisoners .

1147
01:03:08,660 --> 01:03:11,621
The name and face were about all Stoker took from the pamphlet

1148
01:03:11,705 --> 01:03:13,537
since it was printed in old German .

1149
01:03:13,832 --> 01:03:17,120
Stoker 's Dracula is a count who has a home in Transylvania

1150
01:03:17,210 --> 01:03:20,123
and a castle near the actual Borgo Pass .

1151
01:03:20,422 --> 01:03:24,461
Vlad Dracula was a prince with his home in Wallachia

1152
01:03:24,551 --> 01:03:27,760
and a castle in Poenari , near Curtea De Arges ,

1153
01:03:27,846 --> 01:03:30,884
nowhere near the Transylvania-Bukovina border .

1154
01:03:31,641 --> 01:03:34,554
Dracula was finally published in June 1894 ,

1155
01:03:34,644 --> 01:03:38,057
in a run of 3,000 copies , and cost six shillings .

1156
01:03:38,148 --> 01:03:41,311
In 1901 , publishers reissued an abridged version

1157
01:03:41,401 --> 01:03:43,938
in a six-penny , yellow covered paperback .

1158
01:03:44,321 --> 01:03:47,689
Stoker approved the illustration on the cover that showed Dracula

1159
01:03:47,782 --> 01:03:52,276
as a white haired military commander with a white mustache , pointed ears ,

1160
01:03:52,370 --> 01:03:57,456
bat wing cape and large bare feet climbing face down the wall of his castle .

1161
01:03:57,542 --> 01:04:01,536
Not at all the vision of the count immortalized by Browning and Lugosi .

1162
01:04:02,589 --> 01:04:05,172
Bram Stoker 's proud , widowed mother wrote to him ,

1163
01:04:05,258 --> 01:04:07,670
quote , my dear , it is splendid .

1164
01:04:07,761 --> 01:04:10,924
A thousand miles beyond anything you 've ever written before ,

1165
01:04:11,014 --> 01:04:15,008
and I feel certain will place you very high in the writers of the day .

1166
01:04:15,101 --> 01:04:19,436
No book since Mrs. Shelley 's Frankenstein or indeed any other at all

1167
01:04:19,522 --> 01:04:24,938
has come near yours in originality or terror . Poe is nowhere .

1168
01:04:25,028 --> 01:04:29,147
I have read much , but I have never met a book like it at all .

1169
01:04:29,240 --> 01:04:30,856
In its terrible excitement ,

1170
01:04:30,992 --> 01:04:34,860
it should make a widespread reputation and much money for you , unquote .

1171
01:04:35,580 --> 01:04:37,162
Many critics agreed with her .

1172
01:04:37,248 --> 01:04:40,661
Punch called it , quote , the very weirdest of weird tales , unquote .

1173
01:04:40,752 --> 01:04:42,086
The Bookman confessed , quote ,

1174
01:04:42,170 --> 01:04:45,413
we read nearly the whole with rapt attention , unquote .

1175
01:04:45,507 --> 01:04:48,966
And the Daily Mail described its impact perfectly when it said ,

1176
01:04:49,052 --> 01:04:53,467
quote , Dracula is appalling in its gloomy fascination , unquote .

1177
01:04:53,556 --> 01:04:56,594
Stoker 's mother proved wrong in one prediction only .

1178
01:04:56,684 --> 01:04:58,269
Even though Dracula went through

1179
01:04:58,353 --> 01:05:00,720
eight English editions in Stoker 's lifetime ,

1180
01:05:00,814 --> 01:05:02,270
he never got rich from it .

1181
01:05:02,357 --> 01:05:05,190
But Stoker 's biggest disappointment regarding Dracula

1182
01:05:05,276 --> 01:05:08,359
was the reaction of his idol and employer , Henry Irving .

1183
01:05:08,655 --> 01:05:12,273
At 10 : 00 in the morning , on May 18th , 1897 ,

1184
01:05:12,367 --> 01:05:14,699
Stoker organized an actors reading of his novel

1185
01:05:14,786 --> 01:05:16,902
on the stage of the Lyceum Theater

1186
01:05:16,996 --> 01:05:19,158
to protect the theatrical copyright .

1187
01:05:19,249 --> 01:05:22,207
The reading lasted an agonizing five hours ,

1188
01:05:22,293 --> 01:05:26,582
had two special programs printed , and featured a Mr. Jones as Dracula

1189
01:05:26,673 --> 01:05:29,040
and Ellen Terry 's daughter Edith as Mina .

1190
01:05:29,300 --> 01:05:31,962
The audience consisted of friends of the cast ,

1191
01:05:32,053 --> 01:05:35,842
members of the Lyceum cast and cleaners and the Stoker cook .

1192
01:05:35,932 --> 01:05:37,593
Unfortunately , after the reading ,

1193
01:05:37,684 --> 01:05:40,472
Stoker asked the great Henry Irving what he thought .

1194
01:05:40,603 --> 01:05:43,353
Answering loudly , so everyone in the theater could hear ,

1195
01:05:43,440 --> 01:05:45,431
Irving uttered only one word .

1196
01:05:45,525 --> 01:05:46,936
" Dreadful . "

1197
01:05:47,026 --> 01:05:50,439
But Bram Stoker stayed loyal to the great actor until the end .

1198
01:05:50,697 --> 01:05:56,363
In fact , it was Stoker who closed Henry Irving 's eyes when he died in 1905 .

1199
01:05:56,453 --> 01:05:59,912
" And now , " Stoker wrote , " this silence . "

1200
01:06:00,457 --> 01:06:05,497
In the silence , Stoker wrote another eight full-length books and 17 articles .

1201
01:06:05,587 --> 01:06:10,377
Bram stoker died at the age of 64 , on April 20th , 1912 .

1202
01:06:10,467 --> 01:06:12,333
Most of his obituary in The Times

1203
01:06:12,427 --> 01:06:15,089
recounted his long partnership with Henry Irving .

1204
01:06:15,180 --> 01:06:17,296
With a tiny section devoted to his ,

1205
01:06:17,390 --> 01:06:21,509
quote , particularly lurid and creepy kind of fiction , unquote .

1206
01:06:21,978 --> 01:06:25,642
Stoker left his widow only £ 4,700 .

1207
01:06:25,732 --> 01:06:26,732
But he left the world ,

1208
01:06:26,816 --> 01:06:30,184
perhaps the greatest horror novel of all time , Dracula .

1209
01:06:36,284 --> 01:06:39,993
After Dracula , Browning directed The Iron Man , a boxing melodrama

1210
01:06:40,079 --> 01:06:42,036
starring Lew Ayres as a prize fighter

1211
01:06:42,123 --> 01:06:45,115
with a gold-digging wife played by Jean Harlow .

1212
01:06:45,418 --> 01:06:48,126
After that , Browning 's friend Harry Earles , the midget ,

1213
01:06:48,213 --> 01:06:51,006
who played the baby in both versions of The Unholy Three ,

1214
01:06:51,090 --> 01:06:55,755
made Browning aware of a story called Spurs by Clarence Todd Robbins .

1215
01:06:55,845 --> 01:06:58,883
Browning took the story to Irving Thalberg at MGM .

1216
01:06:58,973 --> 01:07:02,435
Thalberg wanted to make a horror film to outdo Universal 's Frankenstein .

1217
01:07:02,519 --> 01:07:06,012
And so , he allowed Browning to make his disturbing masterpiece

1218
01:07:06,105 --> 01:07:08,722
about the circus sideshow , Freaks .

1219
01:07:08,816 --> 01:07:12,025
Browning 's production completely upset the MGM lot ,

1220
01:07:12,153 --> 01:07:14,895
mostly because the studio employees objected to sharing

1221
01:07:14,989 --> 01:07:18,152
the commissary with the actual freaks Browning used in the film .

1222
01:07:18,535 --> 01:07:21,994
This behavior , of course , reinforces Browning 's point in the story .

1223
01:07:22,080 --> 01:07:25,664
After nine weeks , Browning finished the film , and it was previewed .

1224
01:07:26,334 --> 01:07:29,702
Shocked audiences in 1932 were completely unprepared

1225
01:07:29,796 --> 01:07:32,163
for the graphic but very compassionate portrayal

1226
01:07:32,257 --> 01:07:33,841
of the deformed sideshow players ,

1227
01:07:33,925 --> 01:07:36,883
not to mention the terrifying Grand Guignol finale

1228
01:07:36,970 --> 01:07:38,596
when they take their hideous revenge

1229
01:07:38,680 --> 01:07:40,780
on the normal people who have wronged them .

1230
01:07:40,974 --> 01:07:44,183
Reviled upon release , the film was quickly shelved .

1231
01:07:44,269 --> 01:07:47,978
MGM eventually sold it to exploitation distributor Dwain Esper ,

1232
01:07:48,064 --> 01:07:52,524
who rented independent theaters to show it and took all the box office receipts .

1233
01:07:52,610 --> 01:07:56,103
Great Britain banned Freaks from any screening for over 30 years .

1234
01:07:56,489 --> 01:08:00,232
Freaks remains Browning 's greatest film in a career of great films .

1235
01:08:00,326 --> 01:08:02,870
Touching , sometimes funny , and ultimately horrifying ,

1236
01:08:02,954 --> 01:08:05,831
it pleads for understanding and for the humanity to treat those

1237
01:08:05,915 --> 01:08:08,452
who are born different with dignity and respect .

1238
01:08:08,918 --> 01:08:12,046
Despite the controversy , and box office disappointment of Freaks ,

1239
01:08:12,130 --> 01:08:14,292
the film did not destroy Browning 's career

1240
01:08:14,382 --> 01:08:16,544
as some historians would like us to believe .

1241
01:08:16,634 --> 01:08:19,797
He followed it with another movie for MGM called Fast Workers ,

1242
01:08:19,887 --> 01:08:22,879
about a construction crew and their marital problems .

1243
01:08:22,974 --> 01:08:24,806
Browning 's next film , again for MGM ,

1244
01:08:24,892 --> 01:08:27,850
his home during his most artistically fruitful years ,

1245
01:08:27,937 --> 01:08:30,975
was a remake of his silent hit , London After Midnight .

1246
01:08:31,232 --> 01:08:33,348
Tentatively titled Vampires of Prague ,

1247
01:08:33,443 --> 01:08:35,935
but eventually released as Mark of the Vampire ,

1248
01:08:36,029 --> 01:08:40,193
its best moments replay the atmospheric scenes from Dracula ,

1249
01:08:40,283 --> 01:08:43,526
even including Bela Lugosi as the now mute vampire .

1250
01:08:43,870 --> 01:08:47,704
Browning has Lugosi walk through another spider 's web without disturbing it ,

1251
01:08:47,790 --> 01:08:50,077
this time , from the vampire 's point of view .

1252
01:08:50,168 --> 01:08:51,750
More close shots of vermin

1253
01:08:51,836 --> 01:08:54,954
crawling about the vampire 's house uneased the audience .

1254
01:08:55,048 --> 01:08:57,790
And Lionel Barrymore speaks and behaves very similarly

1255
01:08:57,884 --> 01:09:00,501
to Edward Van Sloan 's Professor Van Helsing .

1256
01:09:00,595 --> 01:09:04,304
James Wong Howe 's cinematography rivals Freund 's in many places

1257
01:09:04,390 --> 01:09:07,052
and the art direction of the vampire 's Gothic home

1258
01:09:07,143 --> 01:09:10,593
and the drawing rooms and boudoirs of the victims is almost identical .

1259
01:09:10,938 --> 01:09:14,233
Unfortunately , Mark of the Vampire , like the original London After Midnight ,

1260
01:09:14,317 --> 01:09:16,986
reveals the vampires at the end to be actors in an elaborate

1261
01:09:17,070 --> 01:09:20,108
and implausible scheme to get a murderer to confess .

1262
01:09:20,198 --> 01:09:23,281
But before the climax , Browning indulges so much

1263
01:09:23,368 --> 01:09:27,487
his gifts for creating an uncanny atmosphere and dark fairytale ambience ,

1264
01:09:27,580 --> 01:09:31,323
that the entertaining movie survives its disappointing trick ending .

1265
01:09:31,626 --> 01:09:34,126
The recollections of ace makeup man William Tuttle ,

1266
01:09:34,212 --> 01:09:37,750
give an idea of Browning 's perfectionism on Mark of the Vampire .

1267
01:09:37,924 --> 01:09:41,758
He said , quote , the crew and I didn 't like to work for director Tod Browning .

1268
01:09:41,844 --> 01:09:44,638
We would try to escape from being assigned one of his productions

1269
01:09:44,722 --> 01:09:45,973
because he would overwork us

1270
01:09:46,057 --> 01:09:48,719
until we were ready to drop from exhaustion .

1271
01:09:48,810 --> 01:09:51,518
As far as Browning was concerned , he was ruthless .

1272
01:09:51,604 --> 01:09:54,847
He was determined to get everything he could on film .

1273
01:09:54,941 --> 01:09:58,104
If the crew didn 't do something right , Browning would grumble ,

1274
01:09:58,194 --> 01:10:00,276
" Mr. Chaney would 've done it better . "

1275
01:10:00,363 --> 01:10:01,774
He was hard to please .

1276
01:10:01,864 --> 01:10:05,198
I remember he gave the special effects men a hard time

1277
01:10:05,284 --> 01:10:08,151
because they weren 't working the mechanical bats properly .

1278
01:10:08,246 --> 01:10:09,872
Thought he didn 't drive his actors as hard ,

1279
01:10:09,956 --> 01:10:12,869
he gave Lionel Barrymore a difficult time during a scene .

1280
01:10:13,209 --> 01:10:16,167
Lugosi 's performance , however , satisfied Browning .

1281
01:10:16,254 --> 01:10:18,088
Lugosi did his own makeup for the film .

1282
01:10:18,172 --> 01:10:20,672
I added the bullet hole wound in his head , unquote .

1283
01:10:25,012 --> 01:10:27,754
When Renfield is thrown down the stairs ,

1284
01:10:27,849 --> 01:10:32,639
his scream was cut in the 1938 censored print .

1285
01:10:32,729 --> 01:10:34,845
It 's been restored for this version .

1286
01:10:39,026 --> 01:10:40,937
Mina ! Mina !

1287
01:10:46,159 --> 01:10:47,900
Now , here at the climax ,

1288
01:10:47,994 --> 01:10:51,362
Melford again blunts the suspense in the Spanish version .

1289
01:10:51,456 --> 01:10:54,414
Browning doesn 't let us see Dracula avoid biting Mina

1290
01:10:54,500 --> 01:10:56,332
in his run from the sunlight .

1291
01:10:56,419 --> 01:11:00,504
Melford actually even shows Mina wander away from Dracula

1292
01:11:00,590 --> 01:11:03,298
after Dracula has left her for his coffin .

1293
01:11:03,384 --> 01:11:06,092
Thus , there is no possibility as there is in Browning ,

1294
01:11:06,179 --> 01:11:09,592
that Mina is in the other coffin next to Dracula , dead .

1295
01:11:10,099 --> 01:11:12,682
Van Helsing and Harker , in the English version ,

1296
01:11:12,769 --> 01:11:14,009
assume Mina is dead ,

1297
01:11:14,103 --> 01:11:17,687
and Harker goes off to find something to drive a stake into Dracula .

1298
01:11:17,982 --> 01:11:22,977
Browning doesn 't show us Mina until the stake is driven into Dracula 's heart ,

1299
01:11:23,070 --> 01:11:25,983
revealing that she 's alive , but still under his power ,

1300
01:11:26,073 --> 01:11:29,441
by having her clutch at her own heart with the sound of every blow .

1301
01:11:29,744 --> 01:11:33,237
This also motivates the cut away from Dracula 's death ,

1302
01:11:33,331 --> 01:11:37,245
which , censorship in 1931 , would never allow us to see .

1303
01:11:37,627 --> 01:11:40,745
Again , Browning proves himself the superior filmmaker .

1304
01:11:41,130 --> 01:11:45,044
Browning 's final horror film for MGM was the Devil-Doll in 1936 ,

1305
01:11:45,134 --> 01:11:47,091
the year after Mark of the Vampire .

1306
01:11:47,178 --> 01:11:50,591
Loosely based on Abraham Merritt 's novel , Burn , Witch , Burn ! ,

1307
01:11:50,681 --> 01:11:53,799
it was adapted into a screen story by Browning himself

1308
01:11:53,893 --> 01:11:57,352
and turned into a screenplay by Guy Endore , Garrett Fort ,

1309
01:11:57,438 --> 01:11:59,554
and no less than Erich von Stroheim .

1310
01:11:59,649 --> 01:12:02,061
Lionel Barrymore plays a character reminiscent

1311
01:12:02,151 --> 01:12:04,939
of Lon Chaney 's criminal in The Unholy Three ,

1312
01:12:05,029 --> 01:12:07,441
a man who pursues his illegal activities

1313
01:12:07,532 --> 01:12:10,320
disguised as a harmless old lady running a shop .

1314
01:12:10,409 --> 01:12:13,036
Seeking revenge on the three business partners who framed him ,

1315
01:12:13,120 --> 01:12:17,239
he escapes from prison and uses the formula from a mad scientist

1316
01:12:17,333 --> 01:12:19,540
to reduce humans to doll size .

1317
01:12:19,627 --> 01:12:23,040
He learns to will these devil dolls to kill his enemies .

1318
01:12:23,130 --> 01:12:25,299
The most cinematic of Browning 's sound films ,

1319
01:12:25,383 --> 01:12:28,045
The Devil-Doll boasts clever special effects ,

1320
01:12:28,135 --> 01:12:31,628
great oversized sets for the killer dolls to romp in ,

1321
01:12:31,722 --> 01:12:33,804
and brilliant directorial design .

1322
01:12:33,891 --> 01:12:37,225
Many of Browning 's themes such as the passion for revenge ,

1323
01:12:37,311 --> 01:12:40,929
crippled characters , and the final isolation of the tragic anti-hero ,

1324
01:12:41,023 --> 01:12:43,185
show up for the last time .

1325
01:12:44,819 --> 01:12:46,059
She is not here .

1326
01:12:47,280 --> 01:12:48,361
Then

1327
01:12:48,531 --> 01:12:50,067
then she may be alive !

1328
01:12:51,200 --> 01:12:52,907
Mina ! Mina !

1329
01:12:54,120 --> 01:12:55,201
Mina !

1330
01:13:01,210 --> 01:13:02,291
Mina !

1331
01:13:07,425 --> 01:13:10,759
In 1938 , Dracula was re-released , but by then ,

1332
01:13:10,845 --> 01:13:13,212
the production code had gone into effect .

1333
01:13:13,306 --> 01:13:17,641
The groans that Bela Lugosi omits when the stake is driven into his heart

1334
01:13:17,727 --> 01:13:21,641
were censored from three or four groans , down to one very weak one .

1335
01:13:21,731 --> 01:13:24,940
We 've restored all the groans back to this print .

1336
01:13:25,860 --> 01:13:26,941
Mina !

1337
01:13:27,445 --> 01:13:28,526
Mina !

1338
01:13:30,323 --> 01:13:31,529
Oh , John !

1339
01:13:31,616 --> 01:13:32,981
John , darling !

1340
01:13:33,868 --> 01:13:35,575
Three years after The Devil-Doll ,

1341
01:13:35,661 --> 01:13:39,871
in 1939 Browning made his last film , Miracles for Sale ,

1342
01:13:40,124 --> 01:13:44,664
again for MGM , a murder melodrama with some striking Browning touches .

1343
01:13:45,046 --> 01:13:48,289
The wealthy director then retired to live in Santa Monica .

1344
01:13:48,382 --> 01:13:51,875
Five years later , Variety mistakenly printed his obituary .

1345
01:13:51,969 --> 01:13:55,597
But like the hypnotic living corpse that Browning used to play in carnivals ,

1346
01:13:55,681 --> 01:13:59,640
he survived his own supposed burial and lived until 1962 .

1347
01:13:59,977 --> 01:14:03,641
He left a legacy of American horror films , both silent and sound ,

1348
01:14:03,731 --> 01:14:05,597
that were personal and quirky ,

1349
01:14:05,691 --> 01:14:07,853
but somehow tapped into the strange corners

1350
01:14:07,944 --> 01:14:10,732
of every mind in his fascinated audience .

1351
01:14:10,821 --> 01:14:12,812
He never worked harder than on Dracula ,

1352
01:14:12,907 --> 01:14:17,242
and his success is proved by the fact that we 're still watching and analyzing

1353
01:14:17,328 --> 01:14:19,990
its misty poetry almost a century later .

